{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n '\"'^#|'\"\\\\| (F8|F9|F10|I1|L8|DX7|Zoom|A[0-9]|P[0-9]|DX[0-9]|F[0-9]|L[0-9])' docs/02-product-spec.md docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md; cat docs/decisions/0004-tooltip-and-focused-text.md\"","aggregated_output":"docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:1:# Phase 2 plan: Axylo 1.0 in TypeScript\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:9:## 1. Public API\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:13:### 1.1 Shape\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:38:### 1.2 Data: one logical table, three forms\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:63:### 1.3 Fields\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:90:### 1.4 Common options\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:107:### 1.5 Handle and commit contract\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:130:### 1.6 Diagnostics\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:149:### 1.7 React adapter\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:167:## 2. Architecture\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:169:### 2.1 Pipeline\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:190:### 2.2 Invalidation\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:204:### 2.3 Rendering\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:216:### 2.4 Document structure\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:239:### 2.5 Repository\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:255:## 3. Milestones\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:259:### M0. Foundations (ceiling: 5 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:268:### M1. A vertical slice, and the two open decisions (ceiling: 12 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:284:### M2. Faithful data, on the line chart (ceiling: 8 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:296:### M3. Reading the chart (ceiling: 10 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:309:### M4. Accessibility, complete on the line chart (ceiling: 10 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:322:### M5. Bar, area, donut, scatter and the compact variant (ceiling: 18 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:336:### M6. Updates and scale (ceiling: 10 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:348:### M7. Distribution and documentation (ceiling: 8 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:359:### M8. Release (ceiling: 10 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:370:## 4. Test strategy\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:378:| Accessibility | axe-core, accessibility-tree snapshots, keyboard scripts, recorded rehearsal | A1–A10. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:386:## 5. Risks\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:391:| DOM text costs too much with many charts on a page. | P5 is measured in M1. A fallback to canvas-drawn tick labels would give up user text spacing and forced-colors adaptation for those labels, so it must pass the same L1 and A6 tests before it is allowed. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:394:| Layout promises collide in small tiles. | L8 defines the fallback; the property test covers down to 240 px. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:398:## 6. What waits until after 1.0\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:1:# Phase 1b: Axylo product specification\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:7:## 1. What Axylo is\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:15:### 1.1 Why someone would choose it\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:29:### 1.2 Who it is for\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:35:### 1.3 Principles\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:46:## 2. Scope of the first release\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:48:### 2.1 Chart types\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:65:### 2.2 Workflows\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:76:### 2.3 Supported environments\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:86:## 3. Acceptance criteria: the reader's experience\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:90:### 3.1 Faithful representation\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:94:| F1 | Bar and area value axes always include zero. An author `domain` that excludes zero is widened and raises AX110. | Property test on domains, including all-negative and mixed-sign data; `bars` fixture (values 82–97) starts at 0 | R1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:95:| F2 | Missing values are never drawn as zero and never bridged unless the author asks. An explicit `null` is a gap, silently. An isolated value between two gaps is a visible point. A missing bar shows a \"no data\" mark at the baseline, distinct from a zero bar. On a time or date axis with a regular step, an absent step is a gap and raises AX105; `missing: 'zero'` on the field turns absent and null values into zeros for counts. Tooltip, table and announcements say \"No data\". | `trend` with nulls and `trend` with the same 7 rows deleted render the same gap; `dirty` fixtures; property test that no segment connects across a missing index | R5 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:96:| F3 | Input that cannot be plotted as given is reported, never guessed. The diagnostic registry (section 3.5) fixes each case's level, what is drawn and what the reader sees. | One test per code; the combined `dirty` fixture and the four one-bad-value fixtures yield exactly the codes listed in section 3.5 | R5, D5 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:97:| F4 | Three temporal types, never mixed silently. **Calendar dates** (`2026-02-24`, `2026-02`, `2026`) read the same in every viewer time zone. **Instants** (epoch milliseconds declared `type: 'time'`, `Date` objects, ISO strings with an offset) are shown in `timeZone` (default: the viewer's), with the zone named in the tooltip heading and the description. **Wall-clock times** (date-time strings without an offset) are shown as written, with hint AX112. Instants that all fall on the same time of day, whole days apart, raise hint AX111 (\"these look like calendar dates\"). A bare number is never assumed to be an instant. | Four fixture variants in UTC, America/Los_Angeles and Asia/Kolkata: (a) date strings: identical text in all three; (b) ISO strings with `Z`, (c) epoch ms with `type: 'time'`, (d) `Date` objects: each zone's correct local text, zone visible, AX111 raised; with `timeZone: 'UTC'` or `type: 'date'`: identical. Plus: both DST transitions, a leap day, an unparseable date | R3 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:98:| F5 | No smoothing. When a line is denser than the screen, the drawing is simplified with this guarantee: for every device-pixel column, and every contiguous run of valid samples inside it, the drawn stroke covers exactly that run's minimum and maximum. A break between two runs is always kept, even when both fall in one pixel column; a slot a series merely has no row for (weekly data beside daily) does not break its line. Stacked areas are simplified on their cumulative sums. Exact values always come from the data, not the drawing. | Assertions on which source samples the drawing kept, for: one outlier in 1,000,000; two peaks and a gap in one column; alternating valid and missing samples; tied extremes; device pixel ratio 1 and 2; several zoom levels. A sample that the drawing dropped is still reachable by keyboard and in the table | D8, D9 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:99:| F6 | Numbers and dates are formatted for the page locale. Ticks and readouts are formatted separately: ticks may be compact (`50K`); tooltip, table and announcements show the full integer part, and the fraction with up to six significant digits not counting the fraction's leading zeros (`123,456.75`; `0.000123456`; `0.1 + 0.2` reads `0.3`), with the unit. A custom formatter that throws falls back to the default and raises a diagnostic. | Expected strings for chosen values (large integers, small decimals, percentages, zoned times) in `en-US`, `de-DE` and `en-IN` | R4 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:100:| F7 | Domains follow one order of precedence: the zoomed view is inside the author's `domain` if given, otherwise inside the automatic domain. The automatic domain contains all data, reference lines and stack totals, and ends at the first tick at or beyond each extreme. A field declared `scale: 'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1) ends at full scale only when its maximum is at least half of full scale and not above it. Nothing is inferred from a label. | Property tests; `bars` ends at 100 when declared `percent`; a 4% maximum ends at the first tick at or above 4% | R1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:101:| F8 | Donut: slices keep the given order, start at 12 o'clock and run clockwise. The legend shows every slice's value and percentage as text, in slice order. A zero slice is listed without an arc; a `null` slice is listed as \"No data\". A negative value is AX120 and the chart shows its error state. With more than eight slices that have an arc (a positive value), the seven largest are drawn and the rest of those are grouped as \"Other (n)\", with AX121; zero and `null` slices never count toward the limit and are never grouped, so each stays listed on its own; the table lists every category. Shares are shown with at most one fraction digit. An all-zero donut shows the empty state. | `parts` fixture; one test per case | R7, landscape scoreboard 3.1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:102:| F9 | Up to eight series, no two share a color. Beyond eight, each series still has a unique combination of color and dash or marker (for bars: color and fill pattern, which gives 32 distinct combinations; beyond 32 bar series combinations repeat), and AX130 is raised. | Tests with 6, 8 and 12 series | R7 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:103:| F10 | Stacks place positive and negative values on their own sides of zero, and the axis covers both totals. The readout's total is the sum when every component has the same sign; when signs are mixed it gives the positive total and the negative total, the two ends the reader sees, not their net. A missing component is drawn with zero height and the stack is marked \"incomplete\" in tooltip, table and announcements. | Property test on stacked geometry; mixed-sign and incomplete fixtures | R5 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:104:| F11 | Line, area and scatter are drawn in x order whatever the input order. On line, area and bar, two rows with the same x in one series are AX108 and that series is not drawn. On scatter, points sharing an x (or both coordinates) are ordinary data. | Shuffled `trend` renders identically; duplicate fixture | R5 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:105:| F12 | When a warning means values were left out or changed, the chart says so to the reader: a short note (\"Values not shown: 2. See table.\", worded so it reads correctly for any count) inside the figure and in the accessibility tree, in every build. | Production build given `dirty` shows the note | R5, R6 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:107:### 3.2 Legibility\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:111:| L1 | No visible text overlaps other text or is clipped, and no label disappears without the reader being told. Remedies, in order: thin (time, date, numeric and `ordered` category axes only); wrap to at most three lines; rotate up to 45°; truncate in the middle, keeping every label distinct, with the full text in tooltip and table. If labels still do not fit, a visible note (\"Some labels hidden. See table.\") appears and AX143 suggests horizontal bars. Axylo never changes a chart's orientation on its own. | In real browsers, after fonts load: for widths 240–1,920 px and generated label sets, zero intersecting text boxes (1 px tolerance), zero text outside the figure, truncated labels pairwise distinct. Repeated with the WCAG 1.4.12 text-spacing overrides, 200% text-only zoom, and a font that loads late | R2 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:112:| L2 | Text is at least 12 CSS px and at least 4.5:1 against what is behind it, including text drawn inside a mark and legend entries for hidden series. Marks, axis lines and focus indicators are at least 3:1. Gridlines are exempt: they are not needed to read a value while the tooltip and table exist. Holds in light and dark. | Computed-style and rendered-pixel checks in both schemes | R7, R10 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:113:| L3 | The chart follows its container with no call from the page, in both directions. With no definite height, the plot is 16:9, clamped between 160 and 480 CSS px tall, and the title, controls and table flow above and below it. A container that starts hidden or zero-sized draws when it gets a size. | Resize 720 → 360 → 720 px; hidden-then-shown test; `height: auto` container is stable over 10 frames | R16, D1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:114:| L4 | Light or dark is taken from the chart element's computed `color-scheme`; if that is not set, from the luminance of the inherited text color. `--axylo-background` overrides both. Marks repaint within one frame when the scheme, forced colors, contrast preference, or the `class`, `style` or `data-theme` of `<html>` or `<body>` changes. Forced-colors mode uses system colors. | A page that sets `color-scheme`; a class-toggled dark theme without it; a light-only page on a dark device (must stay light); a runtime toggle; forced-colors emulation | R10 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:115:| L5 | A marker on every point appears only when adjacent points are at least 12 px apart (provisional value), and always for isolated points. | `trend` at 720 px has none; a 12-point line has them | R8 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:116:| L6 | Title, subtitle and axis titles with units are part of the chart. A value axis is titled with its label (the field name when none is given) and unit. An x axis that is a measure (number) is titled the same way; a time, date or category x axis is titled only when the author gives a `label`, since its ticks already say what it is. A reference line or band shows its label next to it. Axis titles never overlap tick labels. | Layout tests; `trend` fixture | R9 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:117:| L7 | Empty data shows a message inside the plot area; so do loading, error and Axylo's own internal failures. No invented axis ranges, no blank box. A change of state is announced once, politely. | `empty` fixture: message visible and in the accessibility tree, no tick labels; forced internal error shows the error state | R6 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:118:| L8 | When the figure cannot hold a readable plot (less than 120×80 CSS px for the plot), it shows the title, a short summary (the first sentence of the generated summary, on at most two lines, cut with an ellipsis beyond that; the full summary remains the figure's description) and the table control instead, and raises AX142. | Narrow and short container tests | R2 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:120:### 3.3 Interaction\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:124:| I1 | Pointing anywhere in the plot shows what is there, by the picking rule for the chart type (section 3.6), within one frame at the 95th percentile. On line, area and bar the tooltip lists every visible series at the nearest x; a series with no row at that x reads \"No observation\", distinct from \"No data\". The tooltip stays inside the viewport, does not cover the active mark or any control, can itself be hovered, and lists at most eight rows plus \"+N more\". In a plot too small for it (decision 0004: narrower than 240 px or shorter than 140 px) the same content appears as text in a strip reserved under the plot. Escape closes it until the pointer reaches a different datum. | Hover at 20 positions including all four edges; sparse-series fixture; narrow tile with eight series; Escape test | R15 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:126:| I3 | Zoom on time, date and numeric x axes is reachable four ways: visible Zoom in, Zoom out and Reset buttons that work with single clicks; drag to select a range; `+`, `-`, `0` and Shift+arrows; and pinch when `zoom: 'touch'` is set. The wheel zooms only while the plot has focus, and never when Ctrl or Meta is held, so browser zoom and page scrolling are not intercepted. | Reach a given range by clicks only; by keyboard only; by drag; page scrolls normally with the pointer over an unfocused chart | D7, R12, WCAG 2.5.1, 2.5.7, 1.4.4 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:131:### 3.4 Accessibility\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:137:| A1 | Every chart has an accessible name: its `title`, or the heading named by `labelledBy`, or else a generated name (\"Line chart of Sessions by Day\") with AX140 raised in every build. The title's heading level is the author's (`level`), and it can be hidden visually. Every chart has a generated summary: type, series, x range with time zone, y range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value, and any omissions. `description` adds to the summary and never removes the zone or omission statements. Element ids are unique per chart. | Accessibility-tree snapshot for every fixture; a 24-chart page has no duplicate ids; axe `heading-order` | R11, R13 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:138:| A2 | Keyboard: at most two tab stops while the table is closed, the plot and one toolbar with roving focus that holds legend toggles, zoom, pause and the table control. Every point is reachable in order; the series can be switched; the ends can be reached directly; focus can leave the chart. The focus indicator is at least 2 px and 3:1 and is not hidden by the tooltip. The exact keys and roles are fixed after the pattern spike in M1 (section 8). | Forward and reverse traversal in collapsed, zoomed, streaming and table-open states; focus after hiding the focused series; indicator contrast | R12 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:139:| A3 | Moving focus to a point conveys its text (series, x, value with unit, position) through the focused element's accessible name or a polite live region. On touch screen readers, every point is reachable by swiping, or the table is reachable within two gestures. | Accessibility-tree snapshot after each key; a recorded rehearsal on NVDA with Chrome, NVDA with Firefox, VoiceOver with Safari on macOS, VoiceOver on iOS, and TalkBack with Chrome | R12, R13 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:140:| A4 | \"View as table\" shows the data as an HTML table built from the normalized data when opened: the title as caption, field labels with units as column headers, x values as row headers, values formatted as in F6. It covers the current x range, 100 rows per page, with every row reachable by paging or by jumping to an x value. It sits in a focusable scroll region no taller than the plot, and opening it does not move other charts. | Table content compared with fixture data, including a value near row 900,000 of 1,000,000; a 24-chart page layout test | R13 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:141:| A5 | Series are never distinguished by color alone, in any mode. Each line and area carries a shape marker at its last point and at sparse intervals, repeated in the legend and tooltip; under `prefers-contrast: more` or forced colors, lines also differ by dash. Adjacent filled marks (slices, stacked segments) are separated by at least 1 px of background. The default palette has eight colors, each at least 3:1 on the default light and dark backgrounds, with a minimum pairwise OKLab difference of 0.10 (provisional) under normal vision and simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia. On any other background, Axylo measures the palette against the resolved background and raises AX141 below 3:1. | Palette script scoring the rendered marks of an eight-series chart in both schemes, repeats included; rendered-pixel checks of separators; a grayscale screenshot in which three crossing lines can be told apart by marker | R7, WCAG 1.4.1, 1.4.11 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:142:| A6 | Content reflows at 320 CSS px and 400% zoom without two-dimensional scrolling. User text-spacing overrides do not clip text. | Viewport and style-override tests in three engines | WCAG 1.4.10, 1.4.12 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:143:| A7 | Data arriving does not speak. Announcements happen only for the reader's own navigation, and for a change of state (L7). While data streams, keyboard focus stays on the same datum until it leaves the window. | Stream test counting live-region changes with and without focus; 24 concurrent streams | R12 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:144:| A8 | axe-core reports no violations on any fixture in any state, including `heading-order` and `scrollable-region-focusable`. | CI | R11 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:145:| A9 | A conformance table lists every applicable WCAG 2.2 AA success criterion, the test that covers it, and what the author must supply (a meaningful title or `labelledBy`, series names, units, the page's own structure). It also states the limits in section 3.8. | Published with each release; a CI check that every listed test exists | R11 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:146:| A10 | Every built-in string can be replaced through `messages`. Built-in English text is marked `lang=\"en\"` when the document language is not English. | Test on a `lang=\"de\"` page | WCAG 3.1.2 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:148:### 3.5 Diagnostic registry\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:188:### 3.6 Type matrix\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:195:| Zoom | On time, date and numeric x only | On time, date and numeric x only | On time, date and numeric x only | No | Yes, both axes later; x only in 1.0 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:196:| Simplified drawing when dense | F5 | F5 on cumulative sums | No; more bars than pixels raises a hint | No | No in 1.0; above 50,000 points a hint says drawing may be slow |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:200:### 3.7 Compact variant\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:214:### 3.8 What the evidence will and will not show\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:218:## 4. Acceptance criteria: the developer's experience\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:222:| DX1 | The first chart takes ten lines or fewer, including import, by npm, by script tag and in React. No required option besides data and fields. | Three CI tests that render the documented snippet from the packed distribution | D1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:223:| DX2 | One logical table, three ways to pass it: long rows, wide rows, typed columns. The three forms of each fixture produce the same normalized data and the same diagnostics, with row indexes in the caller's own form. | Equivalence tests | D2 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:224:| DX3 | With typed rows, a misspelled field, a text field on a value axis, wide `y` combined with `series`, and an option that does not apply to the chart type all fail to compile. At run time, for untyped rows, the same mistakes raise AX101, AX106 and AX150. | [docs/api/examples.ts](api/examples.ts) compiles and each marked mistake fails (already checked with TypeScript 7.0.2); runtime tests per code | D5 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:225:| DX4 | Diagnostics follow the registry in section 3.5: stable code, chart name, field and rows where they apply, the fix, and a documentation page per code. | Message snapshots; link checker | R5, D5 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:226:| DX5 | Three methods change a chart: `update` changes options, `append` adds rows, `zoom` sets the view. They apply in call order. Validation and diagnostics are synchronous; drawing is deferred to the next frame; marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer always switch to a new state together. `destroy()` releases everything and cancels pending work. No `resize()` exists. | Sequence tests: replace then append then zoom in one turn; a rejected `strict` update leaves the last valid chart; destroy before the frame; 1,000 create–destroy cycles show no heap growth trend (P7) | D3, R16 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:227:| DX6 | Colors, font and spacing are CSS custom properties with documented names; the chart inherits `font-family` and `color`. `colors` pins a color to a series name so the same series has the same color on every chart. There is no theme object and no positional palette option. | Theming tests, including a 24-tile page where series subsets differ | R10 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:228:| DX7 | Zero runtime dependencies (reconsidered only with a measured comparison). Size targets, provisional until M1 measures a working slice: 40 KiB gzipped for everything, 25 KiB for a page that uses only `line`, 2 KiB for the React adapter, CSS included. | size-limit in CI on the packed distribution | D4 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:229:| DX8 | Importing in Node without a DOM does not throw, and `validate()` runs there. Works under React StrictMode, including setup, cleanup and setup again. Works under a Content Security Policy without `unsafe-inline` or `unsafe-eval`, served with real headers. Labels and formatter output are inserted as text, never as HTML. A target that matches no element throws an error that names the selector. | CI tests | D1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:230:| DX9 | Semantic versioning. A deprecated API keeps working for at least one minor release and six months. At most one major version a year. The previous release's documented examples still compile and run against each release candidate. | Release policy; API report diff; upgrade test | D6 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:231:| DX10 | Every runnable example in the documentation is compiled and rendered in CI from the packed distribution. | Docs build | D1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:232:| DX11 | MIT license for everything, including zoom and accessibility. Third-party notices are verified at release. | Repository; release check | D7 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:233:| DX12 | A coding agent gets the first chart right. Given fixed prompts and trap fixtures (UTC-midnight ISO dates, numeric strings, unsorted rows, missing days, a misspelled field), an agent with only the published docs produces a chart with no error diagnostics that passes the reader criteria. Recorded as agent rehearsal, not as user evidence. | Scripted rehearsal at each release | D1, 1.2 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:235:## 5. Performance budgets\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:241:| P1 | `trend` (3×120), default options, reference desktop | Main-thread task time from the call until the chart is complete | Median of 20 runs | 10 ms | Not comparable across libraries below about 35 ms with the Phase 1 method |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:242:| P2 | One series, 100,000 and 1,000,000 points, typed columns | Same | Median of 20 | 60 ms and 250 ms | See 01, section 5.3: uPlot and tuned Chart.js are the fastest measured |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:243:| P3 | 600-point window, one appended point per display frame at 60 Hz, driven by an independent clock | Main-thread task time per update (per run: task time in the window divided by the number of updates); and frames later than 25 ms | Median over runs; count per 300 frames | 2 ms; 0 | See 01, section 5.3 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:244:| P4 | Pointer moved across a 100,000-point chart | Time from input to the frame showing the matching readout | 95th percentile | One frame (16.7 ms at 60 Hz) | Not measured in Phase 1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:245:| P5 | 24 `trend` charts created on one page, each tile 340 × 200 px | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 200 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:246:| P6 | Download | Minified and gzipped bytes, CSS included | Exact | DX7 | 01, section 1.4 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:247:| P8 | 40 compact charts created on one page, each 160 × 48 px with one series | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 80 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:248:| P7 | Memory | Bytes owned by Axylo beyond the caller's columns for 1,000,000 points; heap trend over 1,000 create–destroy cycles | Measured after forced collection | At most 1× the size of the caller's two columns; no upward slope | Not measured in Phase 1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:256:## 6. Decisions and the findings behind them\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:281:## 7. Changes this revision rejected\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:290:## 8. Decisions still open\n# Decision 0004: where the tooltip goes, and when the focused point's text changes\n\nStatus: decided by the coordinator on 2026-11-18 after the second review of the interaction layer (`reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core3.interaction.review.md`, new findings 3 and 4). Not rehearsed with a screen reader or a screen magnifier; both parts need that before they are called verified.\n\n## 1. The tooltip is anchored to the datum and never takes the pointer (revision 2)\n\nRevision 1 (placed across the reading axis, offset from the pointer, interactive while hovered) was implemented three times and still failed the acceptance tests: because the tooltip's position followed the pointer it moved away when approached, because it took pointer events it stalled the readout or swallowed the click whenever a move landed on it, and it could cover controls and the focus ring. Revision 2 removes the causes instead of tuning distances.\n\n**Problem.** WCAG 1.4.13 requires content shown on hover to stay when the pointer moves onto it. Spec I1 requires the readout to name the datum at the pointer on every move, the tooltip not to cover the active mark, to stay inside the viewport, and to fall back to a fixed readout when it cannot fit. On a line chart the whole plot picks, so anything that takes pointer events inside the plot blocks picking.\n\n**Rules.**\n\n1. **Position depends on the datum only.** For the datum on show, the tooltip is placed against the bounding box of that datum's active marks (all series at that x for line, area and bar; the point for scatter; the slice for donut), enlarged by the focus ring's width. The pointer's own position never enters the computation, so the tooltip does not move while the datum is unchanged, and it cannot run away from an approaching pointer.\n2. **Candidates, in order:** above the box, below it, then to the right and to the left of it (for horizontal bars: right, left, above, below). Along the other axis it is centred on the box and shifted only as far as needed. A candidate is valid when the tooltip lies wholly inside the plot rectangle, inside the viewport and inside every clipping ancestor, and overlaps neither the box nor any control. The first valid candidate is used. For a donut the first candidate is the hole, when the tooltip fits inside it.\n3. **A hovered tooltip never takes pointer events.** It is `pointer-events: none`. The plot keeps receiving every move, so picking never stalls at any speed, and a click goes to the plot and selects the datum under the pointer. Moving the pointer onto the tooltip therefore cannot hide it: on line, area and bar the tooltip stays for as long as the pointer's position along the reading axis still picks the same datum, which a straight approach across the reading axis does; on scatter and donut, while the pointer is inside the tooltip's rectangle the datum is held (computed from coordinates in the plot's own move handler, not by letting the tooltip take the event).\n4. **Rows are not controls while hovered.** A hovered tooltip is text. Clicking or tapping the plot pins the tooltip for that datum; a pinned tooltip takes pointer events, stays until Escape, a click elsewhere, or its datum leaves the displayed frame, and its rows are buttons of at least 24 × 24 CSS px (WCAG 2.5.8) that select a series. Keyboard focus shows the same tooltip by the same placement; it is never over the focus ring because the box includes the ring.\n5. **Readout mode is decided by layout, not by hover.** When the plot is narrower than 240 CSS px or shorter than 140 CSS px, the figure is in readout mode: a strip of two text lines is reserved under the plot in the layout, and the content that would be in the tooltip is written there as running text (\"x: series value; series value; +N more\"), wrapping to the two lines and ending in \"+N more\" when it does not fit. The strip is part of the figure's flow: it covers nothing and nothing covers it. In readout mode there is no floating tooltip. A pinned datum makes the strip's entries buttons of 24 px height.\n6. **When no candidate is valid outside readout mode** (a tooltip taller than the room around a tall stack, for example), rows are dropped from the end and counted in \"+N more\" until a candidate is valid; if none is with one row, the tooltip is placed inside the plot at the candidate that overlaps the box least. This is the only case in which the active mark may be partly covered, and the table still has every value.\n7. Escape dismisses a hovered or pinned tooltip without moving the pointer, until the pointer reaches a different datum.\n\nRejected: revision 1; an interactive hovered tooltip with a \"safe triangle\" (still ambiguous when the reading axis and the approach coincide); always using the readout (loses the association between the values and the mark on plots that have room).\n\nWhat \"can itself be hovered\" (spec I1) means under this decision, for the tests: the pointer can be moved straight from the datum onto the tooltip's rectangle, and the tooltip is still shown, for the same datum, when the pointer is inside it. It does not mean the tooltip is the hit-test target.\n\n## 2. The focused point's text changes only for a reason the reader would name\n\n**Problem.** The plot's keyboard proxy is a native range whose value text describes the focused point, ending with its position (\"Point 4 of 120\"). While data streams in, the count changes on every append, so the text of a focused control changes without the reader acting. Spec A7 forbids unrequested announcements; whether a given screen reader speaks such a change cannot be tested here.\n\n**Rule.** The range's bounds and numeric value are always kept true to the displayed frame, silently. Its value text is rewritten only when:\n\n1. the reader moves (a key, an assistive adjustment, a pointer or touch selection that takes focus),\n2. the focused datum's own values change (the same x and series now read differently),\n3. the focused datum leaves the frame (the text must then say where focus went), or\n4. the plot regains focus.\n\nAn append that only changes the count or the ordinal of an unchanged datum does not rewrite the text. The position in the text can therefore be out of date until the next of the four events; it is recomputed then.\n\nTo check with a screen reader when one is available (added to decision 0001's list): idle appends with the plot focused are silent; an eviction of the focused point is spoken once; an increment immediately after an append lands on the expected neighbour and reads the current count.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/tests-opus.review.md; cat reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/dist.fix.report.md; cat reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/size.analysis.md'","aggregated_output":"<!-- Reviewer: Codex gpt-6.2-astra, effort xhigh (observed in the session rollout, thread 01a1ed47-781f-7770-8b66-5525f5e0b457), read-only, cross-provider. Reviewed commits 13b2f63 and bf8fabf. -->\n\n**The release gate can pass with required behavior removed.** The motion tests disable animations while measuring them. The table tests accept values under the wrong column headers. The reference tests accept a chart with neither reference mark drawn. The distribution suite also passes declarations that fail in a NodeNext TypeScript consumer.\n\nI reviewed `13b2f63` and `bf8fabf`, and checked the five residual failures against `77213c0`. I ran read-only Node, TypeScript, esbuild and font-measurement probes. I did not launch a browser or change files. Browser results below are attributed to the recorded runs.\n\n### Findings\n\n**B** means the test can be cited for a criterion it does not establish. **M** means a major limitation. **m** means a narrower defect.\n\n1. **B · I5 · Reasoned. The screenshot helper suppresses the behavior under test.** Every motion screenshot passes `animations: 'disabled'`. Playwright fast-forwards finite CSS animations and transitions. A one-second entry fade or a transition under reduced motion can therefore produce equal screenshots. `createRaw` also waits for readiness and three frames before the “first” capture. The update test waits two frames, allows another 300 ms, and never requires the updated picture to differ from the old one. An ignored update passes. Capture with animations enabled from the first eligible paint, require the known new data to appear, and measure the 200 ms deadline, including the specified 24-chart change. [Screenshot helper](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/util.ts:246) · [Creation wait](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/util.ts:72) · [Motion tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/i1-pointer.browser.test.ts:419)\n\n2. **B · Packed TypeScript API · Ran. A supported ESM consumer loses the public types.** Both supplied Bundler-mode projects compile. Running the npm fixture with `--module NodeNext --moduleResolution NodeNext --noEmit` produces TS2834 at lines 5 and 6 of the shipped `index.d.ts`, missing public exports, and unused `@ts-expect-error` directives. The React fixture also loses its core option types. The `types` condition is correctly first and `public.d.ts` ships. The failure is the extensionless `./public` inside the ESM declaration. Emit `./public.js` in those declaration references and compile minimal packed consumers in both resolution modes. [Public entry](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/index.ts:5) · [Fixture configuration](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/fixtures/npm/tsconfig.json:3) · [Type tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/types.test.ts:13)\n\n3. **B · DX7/P6 · Ran. The size measurement is reproducible, but CI succeeds over budget.** I recovered the original tarball from the local npm cache, verified its SHA-512 against the committed size record, and independently bundled its production ESM bytes with esbuild `write:false`. Everything measured **283,389 minified bytes, 100,093 gzip bytes, 97.75 KiB**. Line-only measured **282,947 / 99,956 / 97.61 KiB**. CI calls `size` without `--check`. Even with `--check`, the script compares rounded KiB, so 40 KiB plus one byte passes. Compare integer bytes against `targetKiB * 1024` and make that exit status part of the release gate. The later fixture currently on disk gives 98.44 KiB and has a different integrity hash. [Size comparison](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/scripts/size.mjs:64) · [CI invocation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/.github/workflows/ci.yml:44) · [Recorded artifact](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/size-latest.json:4)\n\n4. **B · L2/A5 · Ran the colour math, reasoned about the browser assertions. The palette test measures CSS colours before painting.** `seriesColors` takes the first computed `color` per SVG slot and fills a separate one-pixel canvas with it. The background comes from a CSS variable. Making the marks nearly transparent, changing their actual fill, or covering them leaves those readings intact. L2 similarly walks ancestor background colours, misses a mark painted behind text, and can pass an empty axis-colour loop. The severity-1 Machado coefficients, sRGB decoding, linear-RGB multiplication, clamp and OKLab transform are correct. My primary-colour and white probes matched reference OKLab values. The helper computes the continuous result and omits the simulated-image byte rounding distinguished in decision 0003. Sample known mark interiors and their actual backdrops in both schemes, assert samples exist, and score both analytic and raster variants. [Palette test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a5-not-colour-alone.browser.test.ts:294) · [Colour source](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/util.ts:276) · [L2](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/l-legibility.browser.test.ts:100) · [Math](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/vision.ts:11)\n\n5. **B · A5 · Ran the separator counterexample, reasoned about grayscale. Both perceptual thresholds can give false passes.** I fed `separated` a transition with 0.80 pixel of background coverage. It reported one transition and zero touching. Stacked bars have an additional geometry check. Grouped bars, areas and donuts lack that protection, and the four-slice test accepts three transitions, missing the closing seam. The 85% marker intersection-over-union threshold is invented. Identical shapes can produce different thresholded masks through luminance, subpixel alignment or nearby ink. Check a full CSS pixel normal to each boundary, including the closing seam, and calibrate raster tolerance with known-width controls. For grayscale, include identical-shape negative controls and verify that the crossing traces can be matched to their legend markers. [Coverage calculation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a5-not-colour-alone.browser.test.ts:178) · [Closing seam](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a5-not-colour-alone.browser.test.ts:265) · [Marker masks](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a5-not-colour-alone.browser.test.ts:335)\n\n6. **B · L1/L8/A6 · Ran the text helper, reasoned about layout. A chart can discard labels and still pass.** Removing date or numeric ticks leaves fewer boxes to collide. Only the bar fixture compares a category count. The suite does not establish remedy order, three-line wrapping, the rotation limit, middle truncation with recoverable full text, AX143’s suggestion, or unchanged orientation. I confirmed that two boxes overlapping by 1.1 px are ignored when they have the same owner. Tooltip/readout text is excluded. L8 accepts a visible multi-line summary and does not bracket the 120×80 plot boundary. A6 uses a 320 px Chrome viewport as the 400% proxy and checks spacing separately. Use generated labels with known identities, check clipping ancestors and the actual remedies, bracket the fallback dimensions, and repeat with browser zoom and a delayed font in the required engines. A sibling test already loads an offline font. [L1](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/l-legibility.browser.test.ts:49) · [Text exclusions](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/text.ts:20) · [L8](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/l-legibility.browser.test.ts:419) · [A6](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a6-a10.browser.test.ts:49) · [Offline font precedent](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/render/review.browser.test.ts:439)\n\n7. **B · I1 and pointer/tooltip cells · Reasoned. Late, obscuring or incomplete tooltips can pass.** Every probe settles before reading, so there is no input-to-paint p95 measurement. Scatter and donut supply `marks: []`, making their pointer occlusion assertion vacuous. Bars check only the top eight pixels. The hover test jumps into the tooltip and checks visibility without checking that the same datum stayed put. The narrow readout can drop all eight rows and retain `+2 more`. A scatter radius of 20 px can pass the tested inside offsets and 30 px miss. Exercise a slow pointer path, hit-test the tooltip, check the active mark and all row identities on both surfaces, bracket 24 px, and reverse source order in the tie fixture. Add horizontal bars, hidden area/bar series, and timestamped paints. [Pointer probes](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/i1-pointer.browser.test.ts:37) · [Hover and readout](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/i1-pointer.browser.test.ts:99) · [Scatter radius](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/type-matrix.browser.test.ts:88)\n\n8. **B · A1/A4 · Ran the text oracle, reasoned about the table. Correct words can be attached to the wrong thing.** Swapping North and South’s cells while retaining their headers passes `toContain` for each value. Missing scatter series and donut category/percentage headers can pass the header subset check. The range test omits zoomable bars, and the layout test uses two fixed-height charts instead of the specified 24. For A1, I passed every compact summary-fragment assertion with the overall range statement removed. With an author description, retaining only that text, the zone and the omission also passes. Compare ordered headers and exact ordered cell arrays, assert each named summary clause, and check a date-bar zoom and a 24-tile layout. The fixture formatter also turns `0.000123456` into `0.000123`, contrary to F6. Use hand-written boundary values. [Table expectations](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a4-table.browser.test.ts:86) · [Range cases](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a4-table.browser.test.ts:207) · [Summary assertions](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a1-names.browser.test.ts:104) · [Fixture formatter](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/fixtures.ts:21)\n\n9. **B · A2/A7 and keyboard evidence · Reasoned. The tests can keep reading the old proxy after focus has gone elsewhere.** `valueText` reads the selected DOM element’s `aria-valuetext`. It never requires that element to be focused or exposed as a range. The focus-indicator test compares the tooltip with datum marks rather than the indicator. The streaming test uses a growing window while focused, strips position and donut percentage in one comparison, and omits donut from the stricter check. A stale proxy, a covered ring, or lost focus at eviction can pass. Assert the active element, its unignored AX role, numeric position and bounds, actual ring clearance, and the next navigation result before and after eviction. Keep the DOM value-text assertion explicitly labelled. [Value-text helper](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/util.ts:182) · [Focus indicator](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a2-keyboard.browser.test.ts:246) · [Streaming focus](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a6-a10.browser.test.ts:131)\n\n10. **B · L7/A7/I6 · Reasoned. State changes can reach the reader while the checked surface stays quiet.** L7 verifies one nonempty polite mutation on loading→ready. An unrelated announcement qualifies. Error and internal-failure messages need only differ or be nonempty, with no equivalent bounds, tick or announcement checks. A7’s observer misses a newly inserted, already populated alert whose parent is outside a live region. I6 freezes only SVG scene markup and checks a final row count. While paused, the summary, lookup or open table could advance, or Resume could duplicate one buffered row and lose another. Assert exact state text, geometry and notification sequence, and compare fixture values across the reader surfaces during pause and after resume. [State transitions](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/l-legibility.browser.test.ts:383) · [Live observer](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a6-a10.browser.test.ts:97) · [Pause test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/i1-pointer.browser.test.ts:477)\n\n11. **B · A8 · Reasoned. “Every fixture in every state” exceeds what axe scanned.** The helper requests all default-enabled rules with no explicit rule disablement. Most runs are scoped to `#chart`. The additional `body` run retains only `heading-order`, and the suite discards `incomplete` results without requiring `scrollable-region-focusable` to complete. Hidden scatter series, paused charts, zoomed bars, compact area/bar and other fixtures are absent. The pointer-tooltip setup can also miss a scatter point or land in the donut hole without failing setup. Assert each state exists before scanning, enumerate applicable fixture/state pairs, and record completion of the named rules. Use a page with suitable landmarks for the document-level scan. [State selection and axe results](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a6-a10.browser.test.ts:166) · [Axe configuration](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/a11y/util.ts:117)\n\n12. **B · A10 · Ran the key comparison, reasoned about coverage. “Every built-in string” has a concrete omission.** `seeTable` exists in the reviewed contract and is absent from `MESSAGE_KEYS` and the replacement placeholders. One `ZZ-` anywhere plus the absence of a short English blacklist is enough. Hard-coding `Resume`, for example, passes without entering the paused state. At `77213c0` the language test inspects `.axylo-summary`, while the figure actually references `.axylo-description`. Removing `lang` only from the referenced description can pass. Use a compile-time exhaustive message map, trigger each applicable string, and require its individual sentinel and language at the surface that exposes it. [Replacement and language tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a6-a10.browser.test.ts:215) · [Public message](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/public.ts:204) · [Referenced description](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/figure.ts:200)\n\n13. **B · T-dense/C2 · Reasoned. A spike at the wrong x passes the dense line and area tests.** Both use one unstacked series and compare the globally highest stroke coordinate with a height calculated from the library’s own domain. Moving the spike to another column or drawing every sample passes. No cumulative stack, same-column break, dropped-but-reachable ordinary sample, DPR variation or compact simplification is established. The scatter test counts `M` commands and the donut test counts paths, so repeated geometry can stand in for missing marks. Derive expected per-column extrema and breaks from fixture coordinates at a fixed domain, check stacked cumulative values, and identify marks by location. [Dense tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/type-matrix.browser.test.ts:294) · [Compact fidelity cases](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/compact.browser.test.ts:78)\n\n14. **B · T-limit/F8 · Reasoned. The ninth series can reuse the first cue, and the wrong seven slices can be selected.** The test computes drawn identities but asserts only `drawn.length >= 9`. Multiple elements of one series count separately. Distinct legend SVG markup does not prove distinct painted cues. Every grouping fixture places the seven largest values first, so keeping the first seven passes. The ordinary order fixture is also descending, and the all-zero test accepts an arbitrary nonempty error message. Check eight without the limit warning and nine with it, associate one painted cue with each series, scramble slice magnitudes, and compare the exact grouped arc, legend, table and empty state. [Identity assertion](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/type-matrix.browser.test.ts:404) · [Donut grouping and empty state](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/f8-donut.browser.test.ts:101)\n\n15. **B · L6/T-reference · Reasoned. Removing the reference drawing passes.** The four Cartesian cases verify a domain, label near a calculated line position, and words in the summary and caption. They never require the line or band to be painted. The band is inside the data extent, and its adjacent label is unchecked. The donut negative case likewise checks the label, description and diagnostic without inspecting for an unintended mark. Put each reference outside the original extent in turn, sample its geometry or pixels, and check each label against its own mark. [Reference cases](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/type-matrix.browser.test.ts:429)\n\n16. **B · F2/F12 · Reasoned. Comparing two renderings can preserve the same gap bug, and a changed-value note can say the wrong thing.** For regular dates, bridging both the null run and the deleted run passes the geometry-equality check. The rendered suite never requests `missing: 'zero'`. Scatter counts two marks without identifying them or checking the missing row in the table. The donut F2 body only reads the table. For changed values, `1 … table` satisfies the AX160 case without checking AX160 or the meaning of the note. The AX121 case likewise accepts a note saying the grouped values were lost. Assert independent gap/zero coordinates, the affected rows, the diagnostic, and exact changed-versus-omitted wording at the displayed revision. [Date gaps](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/f2-gaps.browser.test.ts:74) · [Scatter and donut](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/f2-gaps.browser.test.ts:144) · [Changed-value notes](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/f12-note.browser.test.ts:89)\n\n17. **B · I2/I3/I4 · Reasoned. Several control paths can be disabled independently.** Space need only change AX `pressed`, with no post-Space mark check. Zoom out is never clicked, Meta-wheel is absent, and the default pinch can suppress browser zoom while leaving the chart domain unchanged. I4 measures visible buttons and inputs. Selectable tooltip rows are `div` elements and never enter that measurement. Touch exercises a datum and page scroll, leaving most controls unoperated by touch. Assert each control’s visible result with its actual input, check browser defaults and both modifiers, and measure clickable tooltip rows while displayed. Donut and compact are appropriately outside the series-toggle matrix. [Legend tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/i1-pointer.browser.test.ts:200) · [Zoom and touch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/i1-pointer.browser.test.ts:307) · [Selectable rows](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/tooltip.ts:89)\n\n18. **B · L3/L4/L5 · Reasoned. The layout and theme observations permit stale marks.** L3 can resize the CSS box while retaining the old drawing, and never opens the auto-height table to check flow. L4’s dark fixture sets both scheme and text colour, so always choosing by text luminance can pass. It permits two animation frames and lacks independent runtime checks for the specified root attributes and media changes. L5 samples roughly 5.5 px and widely separated points. A 20 px cutoff can pass both. Check a known datum and hit target across resizes, open the table, deliberately put scheme and inherited colour in conflict, measure the first post-change paint, and bracket 12 px. [L3](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/l-legibility.browser.test.ts:159) · [L4](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/l-legibility.browser.test.ts:218) · [L5](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/l-legibility.browser.test.ts:281)\n\n19. **B · C1–C5 · Reasoned. Compact coverage does not transfer across implementations of the three variants.** The general `compact` fixture is a line. Disabling motion or theme updates only on compact area/bar escapes those loops. C1’s hover check only searches for two named surfaces on one tile. C2’s area can fill from a truncated domain minimum to the bottom and pass the vertical-extent check. C3 checks SVG dimensions and the existence of a series element, allowing stale internal geometry. C4 reads cues on the coloured group and misses child paint. C5’s signed corner distances accept a marker entirely outside the figure. Parameterize the relevant fixtures over all three kinds. Verify marks-only behavior, data-derived geometry at each size, child paint, the AX131 suggestion, and a visible corner marker contained within the figure. [Compact fixture](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/fixtures.ts:105) · [C1/C2](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/compact.browser.test.ts:54) · [C3/C4/C5](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/compact.browser.test.ts:130)\n\n20. **B · DX8 text insertion · Reasoned. The same payload in several fields lets one surface vouch for another.** Full visible titles get exact comparisons. Series names, categories, subtitle, field labels, units, tick and value formatters receive payloads, and a document observer detects the selected element types if inserted. But tooltip positives cover only line/bar, the line name can satisfy its formatter assertion, and a table caption can satisfy the whole-table regex while cells are blank. Area has no table-text positive assertion. There is no per-tick, per-unit, summary or exact `aria-*` assertion. Messages overrides and a dirty reader note are never exercised. Use a different inert markup-shaped sentinel for each source, trigger each destination, and assert exact literal text or attribute value plus absence of parsed descendants. [Inputs](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/pages/charts.ts:21) · [Surface assertions](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/csp.browser.test.ts:92) · [Observer](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/pages/observe.js:9)\n\n21. **B · DX8 StrictMode and DX1 scope · Reasoned. A leaked first mount can leave the expected single figure.** The React fixture really enables development StrictMode, but the assertion sees only the eventual figure. Keeping the original subscription or observer alive through cleanup can pass. Observe setup, disposal, setup again and final unmount through the packed adapter, including whether the old handle still acts. The DX1 snippets demonstrate a first **line** chart by all three routes. Only npm is exercised without a title. A matrix that repeats those results for the other constructors needs corresponding minimal examples or an explicit package-level scope. DX10 coverage here ends at those three snippets. [StrictMode test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/first-chart.browser.test.ts:113) · [Untitled test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/first-chart.browser.test.ts:49) · [Snippet inventory](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/snippets.test.ts:13)\n\n22. **M · Fixture isolation and extraction · Ran resolution and archive probes.** Normal core imports resolve inside each fixture, and the React fixture resolves both packed packages. But the npm and script fixtures can import undeclared `@axylo/react` from root `node_modules`. I observed Node resolve `packages/react/dist/index.js`, esbuild pull in workspace React **and core source**, and TypeScript resolve an explicit adapter type reference to `packages/react/dist/index.d.ts`. The existing positive path checks catch several fallbacks in their specific projects. They do not isolate consumers. Put fixtures outside the workspace ancestry and share only declared third-party dependencies. The actual archives contained 10 and 6 regular files, with no links or extended names. The extractor handles ordinary ustar prefixes, GNU `L`, and simple local pax `path=` records. Splitting pax by newline breaks a length-prefixed path containing a newline, global headers are ignored, and symlinks are rejected. npm’s packlist excludes ordinary symlinks. Prefer the npm installation/extraction path or explicitly constrain and test the supported subset. [Fixture placement and extraction](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/scripts/pack.mjs:42) · [Isolation assertions](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/isolation.test.ts:29) · [Type path checks](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/types.test.ts:33)\n\n23. **M · CSP observation · Reasoned. All eight pages have the instruments, but report attribution and style-effect checks are incomplete.** The actual HTTP header meets DX8. The external observer loads before chart code in all four loaders × two stylesheet modes. The negative page requires both script-attribute and style-attribute events, an observed element, blocked execution and at least one POST. It does not match the two reports to those directives. A shared report array is cleared between tests and sampled after 300 ms, so a late POST can be lost or attributed to the next page. Counted adopted sheets can also be empty. Use a per-page report URL, correlate the negative reports by directive/document, and assert a characteristic computed rule and painted result. The linked sheets are same-origin, and constructable sheets and CSSOM assignments are legitimate Chrome paths here. I found no inline stylesheet silently allowed on the eight pages. The documented CDN example has an inline bootstrap and is exercised separately without this header. [Policy and page setup](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/csp.browser.test.ts:52) · [Report sampling and control](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/csp.browser.test.ts:127) · [HTTP server](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/browser.ts:36) · [Documented example](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/examples/first-chart/script.html:6)\n\n24. **M · Distribution oracles · Reasoned. Two tests derive their assurance from what the current source happens to contain.** Removing a development guard makes its body disappear from the guard-derived token oracle, while other detected tokens can keep the check green. The licence test treats finding a copyright header as failure and accepts a notice document that says there is no third-party code. Vendored code without a header passes, and properly attributed code fails. Pin observable development-only behavior independently of the guard syntax. Verify the release inventory and corresponding notices, retaining the mechanical checks for the shipped MIT file, dependencies and imports. [Development token oracle](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/conditions.test.ts:142) · [Licence assertions](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/licence.test.ts:68)\n\n### Disposition of the 14 recorded failures\n\n| Original failures | Judgment from the recorded output and source |\n|---|---|\n| **A10, six types** | **Original product defect.** Built-in English summary sentences inherited German. The later figure code sets a language on both summary elements, and all six pass in the later run. Finding 12 limits what those passes establish. [Figure update](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/figure.ts:200) |\n| **A5, grouped bar** | **Original product defect under the ordinary reading of “adjacent filled marks.”** At the original revision each bar consumed the full group subdivision, leaving zero geometric gap. The reported 0.22 px is consistent with antialiasing at a touching edge. The later revision subtracts a pixel and the fixture passes. [Grouped widths](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/scene/bars.ts:70) |\n| **C4, compact bar** | **Original product gap under A5; residual test defect at `77213c0`.** C4 literally specifies dashes for lines. Requiring a non-colour cue for compact bars is supported by A5. Originally neither of the first two series received a pattern. At `77213c0` the second does, and SVG paints it on child rectangles. The test skips children without their own inline series colour and compares `none\\|\\|` with `none\\|\\|`. Inspect the child fills or pixels before treating this as a product failure. [Pattern selection](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/scene/bars.ts:106) · [SVG paint](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:410) |\n| **L1, line/area/scatter/donut at 200% text** | **Original product defects. Residual reader-visible clipping, with a misleading test diagnosis.** The original output includes full summaries and displaced controls. The later run reports only the first sentence at 240 px. The stylesheet now ellipsizes it, while `Range.getClientRects()` measures the underlying text. That output cannot establish painted ink outside the figure or vertical overflow. Independently, the four sentences measured **270, 276, 303 and 288 px** in local Noto Sans at the fixture’s 24.5 px summary size. Each exceeds 240 px. Under L1/L8 I would retain the reader-facing failure until a meaningful one-line summary fits, and change the test to distinguish clipping, ellipsis and actual overflow. [Summary CSS](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/theme/css.ts:122) · [Range measurement](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/text.ts:36) |\n| **I1, scatter** | **Original product defect.** The recorded misses include a probe inside the plot near the top-edge EU point. `43fc75d` corrected edge projection and grid-cell rounding. The later acceptance run passes. [Grid boundary handling](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/lookup/scatter-grid.ts:47) |\n| **I1, bar** | **Original product defect.** Seven in-viewport top-row probes produced a reader surface outside the viewport. That directly violates I1. The later placement changes pass this fixture. [Viewport assertion](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/i1-pointer.browser.test.ts:82) · [Later run report](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core3.fix.report.md:73) |\n\n### Accessibility, input and waits\n\n**Computed Chrome AX evidence** comes from `Accessibility.getPartialAXTree` and `getFullAXTree`. It covers A1 names/descriptions/heading levels, I2 pressed state, A4 table roles/names/rows/cells and region focusability, F12 note text, C1/C5 image descriptions, and parts of F2, F8, L7, L8, T-reference and A10. **DOM evidence** includes A2/T-keyboard/F2/A7 `aria-valuetext`, live-region mutation counts, nearest `lang` ancestors, and part of the jump-error description check. Those establish authoring or DOM behavior. They do not establish speech. The distribution `figureName` helper uses Playwright’s `ariaSnapshot`, a different mechanism from the CDP tree helper. [AX helpers](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/util.ts:138) · [Distribution name helper](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/browser.ts:166)\n\nI found **no DOM-dispatched synthetic pointer, touch, wheel or keyboard events** in the reviewed acceptance tests. Playwright supplies mouse, keyboard and tap input. Pinch and one-finger scroll use CDP `Input.dispatchTouchEvent`, which exercises Chrome’s input pipeline. Direct `.focus()` bypasses the route to focus, and `chart.zoom()` establishes state without exercising a reader control. Those are appropriate setup operations only when the route itself has separate evidence. Touch emulation provides no physical-phone or touch-screen-reader result. [Pinch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/i1-pointer.browser.test.ts:307) · [Touch scroll](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/i1-pointer.browser.test.ts:385)\n\nI found **no timeout-to-skip path**. The catches after the F12 note, L3 reveal and I4 scroll waits are followed by assertions that fail if the state remains absent. They do allow a transient or substantially delayed success, and I4 should compare the post-drag scroll position with its immediate pre-drag position. The consequential timing loopholes are the multi-frame settle, disabled animations, and the longer observation deadlines. [Note wait](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/f12-note.browser.test.ts:91) · [Reveal wait](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/l-legibility.browser.test.ts:205) · [Scroll wait](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/i1-pointer.browser.test.ts:400)\n\n### The report’s ambiguity choices\n\n| Reading | Judgment |\n|---|---|\n| Space before unit; medium en-US dates | Reasonable English fixture conventions. The spec permits locale-appropriate presentation. The six-fractional-places helper is a separate error. |\n| Summary y range means observed data extrema | Appropriate for a reader asking what values occurred. Test an axis whose bounds differ from the data and require an identifiable range statement. |\n| Scatter “last” means greatest x | Consistent with x-ordered navigation. Use shuffled input and define the equal-x case. |\n| Donut summary gives each value and share | Appropriate for parts of a whole. Also cover grouping, omissions and zero/null slices. |\n| Unmatched table jump gets a visible error | Appropriate. Require the error to identify the field and be programmatically associated with it. |\n| Separator coverage ≥0.75 px | Lowers the explicit 1 px requirement. Antialiasing calls for calibration, not a smaller nominal gap. |\n| Grayscale overlap ≤85% | An unvalidated regression heuristic. It cannot decide whether a reader can follow the crossing lines. |\n| A focused range should not recount arrivals | Consistent with decision 0004 for an unchanged datum. DOM stability is measurable; actual announcements require rehearsal. |\n| AX160 and AX121 count as “changed” | AX121 changes the displayed grouping. AX160 changes the presentation of a value. A disclosure is defensible in both cases, with wording that explains what changed and preserves the distinction from lost data. |\n| Double the inherited font size for text-only zoom | Useful for reproducing a real layout defect. It leaves fixed-pixel text unchanged and cannot substitute for browser text zoom. |\n| Hover the tooltip in one pointer step | Lowers the bar. A reader must be able to move onto it continuously and keep the same content. Decision 0004 explicitly describes that path. |\n| Scatter x-only zoom preserves the y domain | A reasonable 1.0 reading. Confirm the displayed y ticks against a fixture whose visible y extrema change materially. |\n| Allow 300 ms for a 200 ms transition | Lowers the explicit deadline by 50%, before the initial frame waits and screenshot overhead. |\n\n### Type-matrix disposition\n\nThese judgments cover the tested default SVG renderer. A later canvas run had 135 failures, many arising from SVG-specific inspection. It supplies no equivalent canvas pass. [Renderer report](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core3.fix.report.md:99)\n\n| Cell | Line | Area | Bar | Donut | Scatter |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Pointer picking | Nearest-x fixture established | Same | Vertical bands only | Slice, hole and outside exercised | 24 px boundary and tie independence open |\n| Tooltip content | Visible/hidden fixture established | Initial stack total only | Grouped fixture only | Label/value/share established | Coincident row identities open |\n| Keyboard order | Fixture ordinal text exercised | Same | Same for vertical categories | Slice order exercised | x-then-y order exercised |\n| Zoom | Positive zoom exercised | Positive zoom exercised | Category/date covered; numeric and instant eligibility open | Keys tested in a sequence that can cancel; check after each action | x-only domain behavior exercised |\n| Dense drawing | Per-column F5 open | Cumulative F5 open | Count and AX132 exercised | Path count only | Count and AX133 exercised; positions and slow-drawing advice open |\n| Limit | Nine-warning positive only | Same | Same | Sorted-only grouping; boundary open | Nine-warning positive only |\n| Reference | Geometry and band domain open | Same | Same | Rejection/text checked; unintended drawing unchecked | Geometry and band domain open |\n\nThe fixture values and basic arithmetic generally have identifiable sources. I found no basis for alleging that the author pasted browser output. The problematic oracles are identifiable without that allegation: sibling-derived geometry, equality between two potentially wrong outputs, development tokens inferred from current source, and fragment/count assertions that discard associations.\n\n### Verification and areas found sound\n\nMy five read-only Node checks exercised OKLab reference colours, same-owner overlap, compact summary fragments, fractional formatting and separator coverage. The last lines were:\n\n```text\nℹ tests 5\nℹ suites 0\nℹ pass 5\nℹ fail 0\nℹ cancelled 0\nℹ skipped 0\nℹ todo 0\n```\n\nThe recorded browser totals were `14 failed | 443 passed (457)` originally and `5 failed | 452 passed (457)` at the later revision. I did not rerun either browser suite or the dist global setup, which replaces fixture directories. No contract change is needed for the test fixes. [Original report](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/acceptance-browser.report.md) · [Later report](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core3.fix.report.md:94)\n\n- **Colour arithmetic — ran.** Normal-vision OKLab reference values matched, and the severity-1 simulation uses the expected matrices and linear-light ordering.\n- **Input injection — reasoned.** The real-key, mouse, wheel and CDP touch paths are appropriate for the Chrome interactions they actually exercise.\n- **Table paging — reasoned.** All 250 row headers are compared across three pages, and the million-row line fixture independently expects `123.25` at x `900,123`.\n- **Node import and validation — ran.** Both packed core conditions loaded without a DOM, added no globals, and returned AX103 at row 3, AX106 at row 4 and AX102 at row 5.\n- **CSP delivery — reasoned.** All eight variants receive the strict response header and early event listener. The negative control exercises blocked inline script-attribute and style-attribute paths.\n- **Package metadata — ran and inspected.** The `files` lists include the required entries and current declaration closure. `types` precedes runtime conditions. `[\"*.css\"]` correctly preserves CSS as a bundler side effect, and the adapter’s `sideEffects: false` is consistent with its import behavior. The esbuild CSS test alone cannot validate webpack behavior.\n- **Download accounting — ran.** Browser ESM, ES2022, production/default export selection, esbuild minification and gzip level 9 reproduce the figures. Bundler rows count embedded CSS once. The documented script-tag page downloads both its JS and linked CSS, so counting both transfers is correct. The adapter-only figure excludes its peers.\n\n**Criteria I would leave open for at least one applicable type:** **L1–L8, I1–I6, A1, A2, A4–A8, A10, F2 rendered, F5 rendered, F8, F12, C1–C5**, and the partial type-matrix cells identified above. On distribution, **DX7/P6 fails its measured budgets; DX8 and DX11 are partial; DX1 is established only at the demonstrated first-chart scope; DX10 beyond the three snippets is open.** A3 and A9 were outside this review.<!-- Author: Claude Opus 5.5 (requested effort high), Agent-tool subagent a6f0d7846bf125638, resumed. Saved by the coordinator from the hand-back. -->\n\n## Fix pass on the distribution tests (review findings 2, 3, 20–24)\n\nAll seven findings are addressed and every distribution test passes. Finding 2 is a real product defect, now fixed with a two-specifier edit in `packages/axylo/src/index.ts`. Nothing is committed.\n\n### Final runs\nAgainst HEAD `ba11e6f` plus the other agents' uncommitted core changes, with dist rebuilt by `node scripts/pack.mjs` at about 07:25 CET. Final runs were at 07:42–07:47 CET.\n\n- **Node**, `npx vitest run --config tests/dist/vitest.config.ts`: `Test Files 9 passed (9)` / `Tests 106 passed (106)`.\n- **Browser**, `AXYLO_BROWSER=1 npx vitest run --config tests/dist/vitest.config.ts`: `Test Files 4 passed (4)` / `Tests 30 passed (30)` / `Duration 36.27s`.\n- **`npm run typecheck`**: all four projects pass (`tsc -p packages/axylo --noEmit && tsc -p docs/api && tsc -p packages/react && tsc -p tests/dist`), exit 0.\n- **`npm test`**: 11 files fail (77 tests), all in other agents' work: modified `acceptance/f-domains`, `f-donut` and `f-stacks`, plus `acceptance/.scratch/*`, which look like deliberately broken variants. Every failure is a behaviour assertion such as `expected [] to deeply equal ['AX121']`, never an import or resolution error. The three unit test files that import the changed entry, plus `packages/react/test`, pass: 5 files, 139 tests.\n\n### Per finding\n\n**2. NodeNext consumers lose the public types (blocking).**\n- Test first: added `fixtures/npm/tsconfig.nodenext.json` and `fixtures/react/tsconfig.nodenext.json`, and `types.test.ts` now runs both fixtures under Bundler and NodeNext.\n- Before the fix, both NodeNext runs failed with TS2834 at `node_modules/axylo/dist/index.d.ts` lines 5 and 6, TS2459 (types not exported), and unused `@ts-expect-error`. The React fixture also failed in the adapter's own `index.d.ts`.\n- The fix (re-read the file right before editing; nothing else touched):\n  - `packages/axylo/src/index.ts` line 7: `} from './public';` → `} from './public.js';`\n  - line 12: `export type * from './public';` → `export type * from './public.js';`\n- **Not changed:** `packages/react/src/index.ts`, because its emitted `index.d.ts` has no relative specifiers; the React NodeNext run passes once the core's declarations are fixed. The `./core/*` runtime imports in `index.ts` are also unchanged, since they never reach the declarations. No build configs were changed.\n- After the rebuild, `dist/index.d.ts` carries `./public.js`, and all 12 type tests pass. The new specifier still resolves in the workspace type check, in Vitest (the tests above) and in esbuild (`npm run build`).\n\n**3. Size check compared rounded KiB.**\n- `scripts/size.mjs` now exports `exceeds(bytes, targetKiB)`, which compares integer bytes against `targetKiB * 1024`.\n- The measuring code only runs when the script is called directly, so the function can be imported.\n- The header says `--check` is correct but not a CI gate while the targets are under review. I did not change CI.\n- New `tests/dist/size.test.ts` covers the boundary, e.g. 40,961 B against 40 KiB is over.\n- Current sizes:\n\n| Bundle | gzip | Target | `--check` |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| line only | 99.38 KiB | 25 KiB | over |\n| everything | 99.47 KiB | 40 KiB | over |\n| script tag (JS + CSS) | 103.53 KiB | none | — |\n| React adapter | 1.17 KiB | 2 KiB | passes |\n\n`--check` exits 1.\n\n**20. Text insertion.** New `text.browser.test.ts`, served under the strict policy. It runs from the minified script-tag file and from a bundled module under the development condition.\n- **Sentinels:** `pages/sentinels.ts` gives every text source on each of the five chart types its own inert sentinel, `<u data-axs=\"id\">…</u>`:\n  - title, subtitle, description, series name and category;\n  - x and y field labels, unit;\n  - x and y value formatters, and tick formatters;\n  - a compact chart.\n- **Exact assertions:** heading text and accessible name are compared exactly; ticks must match exactly their formatter's output for a number; legend entries are compared exactly.\n- **Containment assertions:** the description, axis titles, tooltip/readout parts and `aria-valuetext` must each contain the right sentinels.\n- **Table:** the caption must equal the title sentinel exactly. Headers and every cell are compared against the fixture row they came from.\n- **Page-wide checks:** every `data-axs` anywhere in the page text or attributes must belong to a whole sentinel, no `[data-axs]` or `u` element may exist, and the observer must have recorded nothing.\n- **Messages:**\n  - A second page overrides every `Messages` key and drives 13 chart states (dirty data, zoomed, untitled, time zone, references, loading, empty, error, ten series, eleven slices, a sparse stack, and the dirty-input note).\n  - It requires 36 named keys, listed by the surface the spec gives each.\n  - The dirty-input note must read exactly ``Values not shown: 3. ${S('m-seeTable')}``, and author error text must appear as text.\n  - Keys that no triggered state shows are listed in `tests/dist/README.md`.\n\n**21. StrictMode and DX1 scope.**\n- **StrictMode:** `pages/spy-axylo.ts` is put between the packed adapter and the packed core through an esbuild plugin. It logs each chart's create and destroy, and which chart's listeners received events.\n- **What the test asserts:**\n  - The log reads exactly create#1, destroy#1, create#2.\n  - The destroyed chart #1 ignores `update`, `append` and `zoom`: still one figure, title unchanged.\n  - Hover events reach chart #2 only.\n  - Unmount adds destroy#2 and leaves `#root` empty. After that, nothing reacts to `update`, resize or the pointer.\n- **Untitled variants:** the npm, script-tag and React snippets each draw without their title and print only AX140.\n- **DX1 scope:** I did not add first-chart examples for the other four chart types. `tests/dist/README.md` and the test header now state that DX1 is established for the documented line chart by all three routes, with only the package-level parts carrying over to the other types.\n\n**22. Fixture isolation and extraction.**\n- **Install by npm:** the hand-written tar extractor and the symlinks are gone. `pack.mjs` now runs `npm install --offline --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund --no-package-lock` in each fixture, from the fixture's own `package.json`. The React fixture now declares `@types/react` and `@types/react-dom`. All of this works offline here.\n- **Environment:** `npm_*` variables are stripped from the install's environment. When the tests were started through npx, one of them (allow-scripts) made the install refuse to run with `EALLOWSCRIPTS`.\n- **Cache fallback:** if the npm cache lacks registry metadata for the third-party packages (a cache filled only by `npm ci`), the install falls back to `--prefer-offline`. That fallback has not happened here.\n- **Guards:** the fixtures still sit under the repository, so isolation is enforced by guards instead:\n  - Node: `tests/dist/guard/node-guard.mjs`, a resolve hook loaded with `--import`; `nodeJson` always uses it.\n  - esbuild: `fixtureGuard` in `pack.mjs`, now on every test build and in `size.mjs`. It immediately caught the CSP module entry's import of the pages directory, which is now explicitly allowed.\n  - TypeScript: the `--listFiles` output, since no option stops TypeScript walking up to the root `node_modules`. I checked: a `paths` mapping does not stop it.\n- **What `isolation.test.ts` asserts:**\n  - npm's lockfile names the packed tarball and its integrity for each of our packages.\n  - There are no links in any fixture `node_modules`.\n  - Without a guard, the undeclared package does resolve into the workspace, so the guards have something to stop.\n  - Declared imports resolve inside each fixture, with and without `--conditions=development`, including the adapter's own `import 'axylo'`.\n  - An undeclared import (`@axylo/react` from the npm and script fixtures, `fast-check` from the React fixture) is stopped by all three guards.\n\n**23. CSP observation.**\n- **Reports:** each page's policy now names its own `report-uri /csp-report/<page>`, and the server keeps reports per page. A last test waits, then requires all 8 pages to have zero reports and no report under an unknown page id.\n- **Negative control:** it waits for exactly 2 reports, requires their directives to be exactly `script-src-attr` and `style-src-attr` with `document-uri` equal to the control page, and the events to name the same two directives.\n- **Stylesheet effect,** on every page:\n  - computed `display: flex` on the figure and `font-weight: 600` on the title;\n  - `--axylo-default-series-1` equal to the light value the packed `axylo.css` declares (#4a94eb), and the first series mark's computed colour equal to it;\n  - a screenshot, decoded by the new dependency-free `tests/dist/png.ts`, with more than 50 pixels of that colour (176 observed);\n  - with the sheet disabled or un-adopted, `display` is no longer `flex`.\n\n**24. Oracles.**\n- **Development-only behaviour:** new `flavours.browser.test.ts`, with a list written by hand from spec 3.5, plan 1.6 and 1.7, and the diagnostics docs. It runs on all four core files and both adapter files:\n  - the AX104 hint prints once in development and never in production;\n  - it reaches `onDiagnostic` and `chart.diagnostics` in both;\n  - the AX140 warning prints once in both;\n  - the adapter's memoize hint appears once in development and never in production.\n- **Token check:** the source-derived check in `conditions.test.ts` is kept but labelled supplementary, with the reviewer's objection stated in its header.\n- **Licence:** `THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md` now has a release inventory of all 16 shipped files, each with origin and licence, plus a Notices section. `licence.test.ts` now asserts:\n  - the inventory equals the tarballs' file lists exactly;\n  - each claimed origin holds: bundle inputs come only from the package's `src`, and the shipped CSS equals the string `theme/css.ts` exports;\n  - any licence or copyright header found in source or shipped files must be quoted in Notices, instead of counting as a failure;\n  - the existing checks (MIT, no dependencies, imports) are kept.\n\n  The notices file now says that third-party code copied into `src` without a header cannot be detected.\n\n### Files I changed or added\n- **Changed:**\n  - `packages/axylo/src/index.ts` (the two specifiers above)\n  - `scripts/pack.mjs`, `scripts/size.mjs`\n  - `THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md`\n  - `tests/dist/{README.md, browser.ts, paths.ts, types.test.ts, isolation.test.ts, conditions.test.ts, csp.browser.test.ts, first-chart.browser.test.ts, licence.test.ts, side-effects.test.ts}`\n  - `tests/dist/pages/{observe.js, page.css}`\n  - the three fixture `package.json` files, and `fixtures/react/tsconfig.json`\n- **New:**\n  - `tests/dist/{guard/node-guard.mjs, png.ts, size.test.ts, text.browser.test.ts, flavours.browser.test.ts}`\n  - `tests/dist/pages/{sentinels.ts, spy-axylo.ts, flavour.ts}`\n  - `fixtures/{npm,react}/tsconfig.nodenext.json`, `fixtures/react/{main-strict.tsx, memo.tsx}`\n\nThe package READMEs and LICENSE files are untouched. No new `.gitignore` lines are needed: the scratch output goes under the already-ignored `fixtures/*/.out/` and `tests/dist/.out/`.\n\n### Not verified\n- The `--prefer-offline` fallback, and fixture installs on a CI runner whose cache was filled by `npm ci`.\n- Windows and macOS.\n- Browsers other than Chrome, and bundlers other than esbuild.\n- The webpack effect of `sideEffects: [\"*.css\"]`.\n- The headless Chrome still running at the end belongs to the other agent's `vitest run packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance`; mine closed.\n\n<!-- Analyst: Codex gpt-6.2-sol, effort max (observed in the session rollout, thread 01a1ed40-1abb-7cc2-8049-290d1bed775e), read-only. Source snapshot 8ab0734 per the analysis. -->\n\n**Keep the feature set and reset DX7.** I would set the next packed-distribution gates at **96 KiB for everything, 80 KiB for `line`, and 2 KiB for the React adapter**. Those are proposed gates. The measured line-only scratch bundle reached **79.79 KiB** with both renderers, streaming, zoom, the table, and keyboard support still in the bundle. With lazy loading, **93 KiB everything and 77 KiB line** are defensible *initial-download* gates. The original 40/25 KiB targets imply a substantially smaller product.\n\n## Measurements\n\nI reproduced the packed measurements from the installed fixtures using the read-only steps in `scripts/size.mjs`. I used esbuild 0.28.2, ES2022 ESM, production conditions, minification, `write: false`, and gzip level 9. For source experiments I froze the source at `8ab0734`, set `__DEV__` to `false`, and substituted code with esbuild `onLoad`. The raw production ESM rebuilt from that snapshot differed from the packed file by 34 minified bytes and 9 gzip bytes. Source deltas below use their own baseline of **99,921 gzip B** for everything and **99,736 gzip B** for the bare `line` export. Source line references are to that snapshot. [Size measurement](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/scripts/size.mjs:33) · [Release build](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/scripts/build.mjs:7) · [Distribution report](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/dist.report.md:60)\n\n| Measured entry | Minified JS B | Download gzip B | Download KiB |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Packed, all exports | 283,389 | 100,093 | 97.75 |\n| Packed, documented `line` example | 282,947 | 99,956 | 97.61 |\n| Packed, bare `line` export | 282,758 | 99,872 | 97.53 |\n| Packed, `validate` alone | 73,998 | 25,556 | 24.96 |\n| Source, `validate` alone | 56,491 | 20,700 | 20.21 |\n| Packed, script tag with linked CSS | 283,869 | 104,274 | 101.83 |\n| Packed, React adapter with React and Axylo external | 2,375 | 1,197 | 1.17 |\n\nThe chart bundles include embedded CSS. The script-tag row also counts its linked CSS. The measured gaps to DX7 are **59,133 B for everything** and **74,356 B for the line example**. [Size cases and accounting](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/scripts/size.mjs:42) · [DX7](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/02-product-spec.md:228)\n\n## Where the bytes are\n\nModule sizes are measured esbuild metafile contributions in **minified B**. Function and table sizes in the middle column are **approximate minified B**, attributed by generated source-map spans. They include nested declarations. Gzip deltas are measured by rebuilding after removing the named behavior and include any callees that then disappear. The deltas cannot be added across rows.\n\n| Source area | Largest functions, tables, and strings | Measured gzip evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Controller** · 23,985 B | `frame` ≈6,499, `update` ≈1,434, `formatSpec` ≈1,034. The frame function coordinates formatting, responsive layout, disclosure, and the atomic publication of marks, lookup, and accessibility state. | Stubbing `frame` removes 27,124 B including downstream stages. Stubbing `update` removes 571 B. [Create and update](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts:492) · [Frame](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts:876) |\n| **Interaction** · 22,278 B | `present` ≈1,342, `build` ≈1,156, `onProxyKey` ≈1,083, `controlItems` ≈882, `placeProxy` ≈880. The shared state links pointer, touch, keyboard, focus, and the displayed revision. | Stubbing `present` removes 1,325 B. Stubbing the whole interaction factory removes 14,272 B including its dependencies. [Factory](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:150) · [Presentation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:360) · [Keyboard](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:543) |\n| **Aligned streaming** · 18,633 B for `store.ts` | `appendInPlace` ≈2,561, `commitBatch` ≈1,664, `batch` ≈1,380, `trim` ≈1,128, `extendSeries` ≈1,062. The shared stream row and aligned buffer modules contribute another 5,944 and 3,307 B respectively. | `appendInPlace` and `batch` ablations each remove about 1,050 B in separate builds. Cutting streaming end to end removes 13,692 B from everything and 11,138 B from the specialized line scratch bundle. [Batch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store.ts:624) · [Append](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store.ts:895) · [Row revisions](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store-rows.ts:275) · [Frame buffers](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store-buffers.ts:23) |\n| **Point and donut streaming** · 7,806 B | `UnalignedStore` ≈3,509, `Points` ≈1,757, `Slices` ≈1,228. The first is shared by the last two. An aligned chart has no use for these classes. | The entire module disappeared from the line scratch metafile once `openStore` directly selected `AlignedStore`. [Point storage](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store-unaligned.ts:87) · [Slice storage](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store-unaligned.ts:175) · [Store constructors](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store-unaligned.ts:424) |\n| **Static data and validation** · 8,692 B normalize, 5,598 B options, 5,715 B x parsing | `XParser` ≈3,237, `resolveOptions` ≈1,240, `pointFrame` ≈1,175, `donutFrame` ≈1,159, `readX` ≈1,074, `alignedFrame` ≈906. The option-name tables include ≈779 B for `messageKeys`. | Stubbing `resolveOptions` removes 1,090 B including helpers. Source `validate` alone is 20,700 B because it runs option resolution, normalization **and** view diagnostics for all types. [Options](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/options.ts:47) · [X parser](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/x.ts:141) · [Frame dispatch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/normalize.ts:465) · [Validation path](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/stages.ts:145) |\n| **Layout and time** · 7,273 B label fitting, 3,461 B layout, 5,107 B time scale | `layoutAxes` ≈1,689, `fitBottom` ≈1,296, `middleText` ≈838. Time-scale `label` and `wallBoundaries` are each ≈660, and the `SUB_YEAR` interval table is ≈543. | Stubbing `fitBottom` removes 720 B. Stubbing temporal ticks, readouts, gap arithmetic, and zone conversion together removes 3,442 B and breaks F2/F4/F6. [Layout](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/layout/index.ts:129) · [Labels](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/layout/labels.ts:363) · [Time intervals](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/scale/time.ts:51) |\n| **Marks and theme** · 9,025 B SVG, 6,307 B Canvas, 4,975 B theme resolution | SVG `createSvgRenderer` ≈3,600, including ≈1,023 for bar-only `patternUrl`. Canvas `createCanvasRenderer` ≈1,633 and `paintRects` ≈1,545. Both use shared geometry. Theme `resolveTheme` ≈1,167 and `validColors` ≈662. The light and dark palette literals together are ≈398. | SVG-only saves 2,190 B. Canvas-only saves 2,975 B. Both keep the HTML text layer. [SVG](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:212) · [Canvas](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/render/canvas.ts:42) · [Shared geometry](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/render/geometry.ts:122) · [Theme](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/theme/resolve.ts:88) |\n| **Styles and text** · 15,140 B stylesheet module, 6,849 B registry, 2,685 B reader messages | The stylesheet template is the largest string. The AX registry table accounts for ≈5,716 B. `defaultMessages` accounts for ≈1,904 B, including the F12 note, summary, keyboard, and table text. | Removing embedded CSS text with the installer retained saves 3,911 B. Replacing registry prose with generic code/field/rows saves 1,866 B. Blanking reader message values saves just 479 B and breaks reader behavior. [Stylesheet](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/theme/css.ts:48) · [AX registry](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/diagnostics/registry.ts:39) · [Reader messages](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/messages/index.ts:8) |\n\n**Shared and feature-specific code.** All full charts need validation, normalization, the figure, measurement and theming, exact lookup, and a reader summary. Axis fitting and references are shared by Cartesian charts. Line and area share dense-line simplification. Area adds `addArea` and `addStackedAreas` in a measured 3,088 B module. Bar adds `barMarks` in a 1,839 B module, plus band projection, horizontal labels, and missing-bar glyphs. Bar and area share stacks. Scatter adds point normalization, its store, point order and a 3,176 B grid module. Donut adds slice normalization, its store, ring layout, `arcMarks`, and its separate legend. The line scratch build eliminated the area and bar scene modules and all the scatter and donut lookup modules. [Line and area dispatch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/scene/index.ts:85) · [Area stacks](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/scene/areas.ts:121) · [Bar marks](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/scene/bars.ts:38) · [Stacks](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/view/index.ts:348) · [Donut legend](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:1149)\n\n**Optional behavior already reachable from every relevant chart.** Table work lives in `buildTable` (≈1,663 minified B), `createTableView` (3,232 B module), and interaction’s open, paginate, and jump functions. The tooltip has a 3,493 B presenter module plus overlay and anchor logic, some shared with keyboard focus. Zoom spans controller `zoom`, focus window arithmetic, and DOM button, wheel, drag, and pinch handlers. Compact reuses the main draw path. Folding compact-only branches to false with CSS and schemas retained removed only **156 measured gzip B**. [Table model](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/reader/table.ts:78) · [Table DOM](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/table.ts:43) · [Table open and jump](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:997) · [Tooltip](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/tooltip.ts:76) · [Zoom arithmetic](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/focus.ts:275) · [Compact layout](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/layout/index.ts:219)\n\n## Why `line` retains everything\n\nThe source metafile showed **zero contributing modules disappearing** when I switched from all exports to the bare `line` export. Only `index.ts` and `core/stages.ts` had lower attributed module byte counts. The production package exports a single prebundled ESM file, but the same result from source establishes the actual dependency problem. `sideEffects: [\"*.css\"]` is already in place. [Package metadata](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/package.json:7) · [Build entry](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/scripts/build.mjs:7)\n\n1. **`line` → `createChart('line', …)` → `createController(kind, …)`.** Area, bar, scatter, and donut call the same functions. The literal passed by each wrapper crosses the same runtime dispatch. The controller keeps its append implementation because every returned `Chart` exposes synchronous `append`, even on a page that has yet to stream anything. Its `openStore` directly references `AlignedStore.fromFrame`, `new PointStore`, and `new DonutStore`. [Entry points](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/index.ts:14) · [Browser host](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/chart.ts:129) · [Store dispatch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts:555) · [Public handle](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts:1427)\n2. **`update` → `resolveOptions` → `normalize` → `buildView`.** `keysForChart[type]` retains all option schemas. `normalize` calls `donutFrame`, `pointFrame`, or `alignedFrame`. `buildView` calls `buildDonut`, does scatter extent and range work, or builds aligned views with bar band and stack branches. Specializing the store alone leaves these static paths reachable. [Option resolution](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/options.ts:209) · [Normalize](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/normalize.ts:465) · [View dispatch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/view/index.ts:304)\n3. **`frame` → axes, scene, lookup, reader, and DOM interaction.** `layoutInput` selects ring, bands, or XY. `buildScene` references arcs, bars, scatter points, area, and line. `buildLookup` switches between donut, scatter, and aligned lookup. `buildReader` calls `buildTable`, which has three frame-shape paths. The interaction factory branches on the same types for keyboard navigation, overlays, swatches, and the donut legend. [Controller dispatch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts:1040) · [Axis dispatch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/axes.ts:79) · [Scene dispatch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/scene/index.ts:85) · [Lookup switch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/lookup/index.ts:8) · [Table switch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/reader/table.ts:78) · [Navigation dispatch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:465)\n4. **`createChart` selects both renderers at runtime.** The private `__renderer` switch directly references both factories. Each factory’s mark switch references every mark painter, including arcs and bar patterns even when the scene can only produce line marks. The chart also directly creates the text layer, DOM figure and interaction layer, and installs styles and watchers. [Renderer choice and browser services](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/chart.ts:60) · [SVG mark switch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:285) · [Canvas mark switch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/render/canvas.ts:160)\n\n**Smallest restructuring that removes those edges.** Give each named chart entry a small private composition that supplies its option schema, normalizer and store opener, view and layout builders, scene and lookup builders, plus the type-specific navigation and reader operations. Keep one controller, one browser host, and one implementation of shared aligned data and Cartesian layout. Pass the aligned store its encoding operations so its re-encoding path can also avoid the all-type resolver. Split the two render switches into mark handlers shared by per-type renderer compositions. A line composition needs paths, points, rules and bands, including the points and rules used by focus. It can omit area fills, bar rectangles and arcs. Keep `validate(type, options)` on a separate, pure dispatch path so importing `line` alone cannot reach it. [Controller view and store calls](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts:416) · [Aligned store re-encoding](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store.ts:460) · [Validation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/index.ts:34)\n\nShip the production and development ESM graphs with those module boundaries intact. The package file allowlist and packed-content tests will need to admit the modules and any chunks. Keep the classic `Axylo` IIFE as a single, self-contained build of all exports. It can retain both renderers and the full interaction layer. The public names, options, handle signatures, and fixed core contracts can stay as they are. [Package allowlist](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/package.json:8) · [Package-content tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/package-contents.test.ts:20) · [Public API](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/public.ts:110)\n\nI tested the dependency result with an in-memory `export { line }` entry. I folded the relevant chart-kind and frame-shape branches to line, replaced `openStore` with a direct aligned call, and removed the unreachable mark cases in each backend. This measured **81,704 gzip B (79.79 KiB)** with both renderers, down **18,032 B**. SVG alone measured **80,321 B (78.44 KiB)**. The metafile confirmed that point/donut stores, the scatter grid and point order, all scatter/donut lookup, and bar/area scene modules disappeared. This models the code the composition can omit. A production refactor will have dispatch and packaging overhead of its own.\n\n## Ranked reductions for everything\n\nThe ranking prioritizes shippable savings. All deltas are gzip B. “Cut” denotes a measured ablation, and effort is estimated.\n\n| Rank | Change and measured saving | Verdict, risk, and effort |\n|---:|---|---|\n| 1 | **Minify the embedded CSS.** Pre-expanding and minifying the packed-equivalent stylesheet saved **1,604 B** in JS. The standalone file went from **4,095 to 2,433 B** gzip. The current script snippet’s duplicate download costs **4,095 B**, or **2,433 B** after CSS minification. | **Do it.** Generate both forms from the same stylesheet. Make the linked file optional in the default snippet, as the README already allows. Moving all CSS to a link adds a setup dependency for DX1 and changes the DX8 CSP and first-render paths. Check L1–L4, A5–A6 and DX6. Small. [Installer](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/theme/styles.ts:29) · [Script snippet](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/examples/first-chart/script.html:3) · [README](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/README.md:48) |\n| 2 | **Keep SVG eager and defer Canvas.** SVG-only cut **2,190 B**. Canvas-only cut **2,975 B**. A real ESM dynamic edge for Canvas saved **1,647 B initially** for everything and **916 B** after line specialization, including split overhead. | **Keep SVG as the default pending the renderer rerun.** Canvas is useful enough to preserve for comparison, especially dense scatter. Decision 0002 voids the old dashboard comparison and calls for dense scatter, zoom, headed GPU and another browser before a final choice. Check F5, A5, L4, P1–P5 and P7. Medium. [Selection](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/chart.ts:62) · [Decision 0002](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md:47) |\n| 3 | **Lazy-load the table.** Splitting only `createTableView` saved **923 B initially** for everything. Cutting its model, presenter, disclosure handlers and jump code together saved **2,923 B**. The latter is the measured amount available before adding loading and availability code. | **Split the presenter first.** Move model construction only after the A4 readiness and focus behavior is tested. Keep the control, table availability, and F12 wording synchronous. Check A2–A4, F2, F5–F6, F12, L8 and DX8. Medium for the presenter, larger for the whole boundary. [Reader model](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/reader/index.ts:148) · [Open, focus and jump](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:997) |\n| 4 | **Remove production instrumentation.** Removing the stage counters, forced-failure logic and benchmark handle block saved **586 B**. `__dev` already disappears. `__AXYLO_BENCH__`, `__bench`, and the forced-stage-failure string were still present in the measured production source bundle. | **Do it with the benchmark owner.** Give the harness its own production-optimized instrumentation build, and run shipped-bundle performance checks as well. Keep AX199 recovery. Check P1–P8 evidence and DX5 failure recovery. Small. [Counters](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts:250) · [Runtime benchmark hook](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts:1445) |\n| 5 | **Shorten diagnostic advice.** Shortening hint text saved **253 B**. Shortening all registry messages while retaining outcome templates saved **774 B** in the scratch build. The **1,866 B** generic-message cut discarded required detail. | **Keep the codes and actionable production messages.** Move expanded examples and explanations into development or the documented code pages. Preserve AX100 lengths, AX101 and AX150 suggestions, chart/field/rows, strict errors and warning delivery. Hints are suppressed in the production *console* but still reach `validate`, `diagnostics` and `onDiagnostic`. Keep each hint’s required meaning and retain reader text and disclosure counts in every build. Check F3, F4, F12, C5, A10 and DX4–DX5. Small to medium. [Registry requirements](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/02-product-spec.md:148) · [Public diagnostic](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/public.ts:163) · [Delivery](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/delivery.ts:50) · [Reader disclosure](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/diagnostics/collector.ts:55) |\n| 6 | **Reuse exact duplicates and trim private class methods.** One consolidation of the message allowlist, searches, clamp, unit label, and identical DOM attribute setters saved **381 B**. Removing store convenience methods and their alternate append wrapper saved **204 B**. Mangling a selected set of internal property names saved **217 B** in a separate build. | **Consolidate exact duplicates.** Preserve the hot search and DOM mutation semantics. Keep names used by user options, `Messages`, diagnostics, CSS, or quoted/dynamic property access stable. Mangling is a low-return follow-up and needs consistent names across chunks. Check F7, A2/A4, DX3–DX6. Small to medium. [Shared searches](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/lookup/search.ts:1) · [Duplicate focus helpers](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/focus.ts:38) · [Duplicate message keys](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/options.ts:55) · [DOM setter](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/figure.ts:74) · [Store wrappers](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store.ts:511) |\n| 7 | **Defer plot keyboard navigation.** Stubbing the proxy handlers, placement, and navigation entry points saved **1,446 B**. I estimate **800–1,200 B** net after a synchronous shell and loader. | **Keep eager for the first release.** The proxy supplies native focus and screen-reader input, and shares the active datum with pointer and streaming. Deferring the first keystroke or announcement is a material regression. Check A2–A3, A7, A10 and I3. Medium to large. [Proxy creation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:220) · [Proxy keys](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:543) · [Revision remapping](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:1252) |\n| 8 | **Separate streaming from static normalization internally.** The **13,692 B** streaming cut removed `append`, the store dispatch, pause/follow logic, and stream seeds from static normalization. | **Keep synchronous streaming in the standard entry.** Loading storage on first `append` would break synchronous validation and ordered calls. Full renormalization per append would put P3/P7 and pause/retention behavior at risk. Share draft/commit and re-encoding logic where the aligned and unaligned paths agree. Large for meaningful savings. [Stream seed](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/normalize.ts:465) · [Aligned transaction](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store.ts:471) · [Unaligned transaction](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store-unaligned.ts:385) · [Commit contract](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:107) |\n| 9 | **Keep calendar and zone arithmetic.** The broad temporal cut saved **3,442 B**. Stubbing just inverse zone conversion and the offset search saved **228 B**. | `Intl` already supplies formatting and zone data. It does not supply strict three-kind classification, civil tick stepping, or the inverse mapping needed to distinguish repeated and skipped wall times. The parser, gap engine and scale already share `utcFields` and zone conversion. Keep them for F2, F4 and F6. Large to redesign. [Parsing](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/x.ts:64) · [Gap arithmetic](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/steps.ts:39) · [Zone conversion](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/scale/zone.ts:42) · [Intl cache](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/format/intl.ts:6) |\n| 10 | **Keep the scatter grid.** Stubbing `scatterGrid` saved **1,308 B** and disabled scatter pointer picking. | A flat scan can implement the same radius, later-row tie and coincidence rules, with a different cost on dense and clustered data. Keep the grid for I1/P4 and the scatter type matrix. The line split already removes all its bytes from line. Medium to replace and requalify. [Grid, grouping and split](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/lookup/scatter-grid.ts:33) · [Picker](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/lookup/scatter.ts:153) · [Type matrix](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/02-product-spec.md:188) |\n| 11 | **Leave typed-array growth alone for size.** Sharing the simple allocate/copy step between scalar observations and frame buffers saved **40 minified B** and **added 24 gzip B**. | `BufferWindow` already centralizes reserve, compact and copy-on-write rules. Preserve independent inspected previews, borrowed columns and retained row provenance. Check DX2, DX5 and P7. Small experiment, larger correctness surface. [Shared window](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store-window.ts:3) · [Scalar observations](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store.ts:83) · [Parallel buffers](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store-buffers.ts:63) |\n\nTwo other cuts show the cost of the remaining feature paths. The tooltip presenter and its presentation branch saved **1,570 B** when stubbed, and disabling zoom handlers and controller zoom saved **989 B** in a separate cut. Keep both for I1/I3. The compact branch cut saved **156 B** with CSS and schemas retained, so C1–C5 are inexpensive to keep. [Tooltip presentation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:360) · [Zoom handlers](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:643) · [Compact criteria](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/02-product-spec.md:200)\n\nThe duplication audit also found distinct checks that should stay distinct. `sameValue` compares option content recursively, `sameKey` compares stage inputs, and the store tests whether a normalized seed is reusable. Field checks recur against initial data, re-encoded retained data, and incoming rows. The `validateOptions` helper in `data/validate.ts` contributes zero bytes, so deleting it would save zero. Text uses DOM setters, and I found no duplicated general-purpose HTML escaping pipeline. The table’s two regex escapes are local to parsing a localized jump value. [Option equality](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/options.ts:66) · [Cache keys](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/axes.ts:246) · [Seed check](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store.ts:30) · [Retargeting](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/options.ts:196) · [Append field check](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store.ts:904) · [Unused validator](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/validate.ts:6) · [Table parser](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:997)\n\n### What lazy loading changes\n\nI built actual in-memory ESM chunks for the Canvas and table-presenter edges. With compiled CSS, their **initial** totals were **93.45 KiB everything / 76.43 KiB line**, and their **all-chunk** totals were **97.55 / 79.54 KiB**, summing the gzip size of each file. The unused eager Canvas re-export and table value import initially caused those modules to land in eagerly imported shared chunks. Removing both static edges fixed that. Compiling the two dynamic edges into one classic IIFE kept one output and **added 530 B** to the comparable IIFE. The single-file script build should keep eager implementations. [Renderer barrel](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/render/index.ts:1) · [Table import](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:32) · [IIFE build](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/scripts/build.mjs:11)\n\nThe npm path can work without author configuration if the build emits relative URLs and deploys every chunk alongside the entry. The existing `script-src 'self'` policy can load same-origin chunks. Cross-origin module hosting needs the applicable CSP origin and CORS. For the table, keep the disclosure control and accessible name eager, resolve the newest displayed revision when the chunk arrives, and make one activation sufficient to open and focus the table. A full model split can use a private synchronous availability check while retaining the pure `ReaderModel` contract for its existing callers. Any keyboard split needs an eager focus proxy and first-action handling. Test throttled loading, failed fetch and retry, updates during loading, and destruction before completion. [Current CSP test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/csp.browser.test.ts:46) · [Reader contract](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts:660) · [Table and keyboard criteria](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/02-product-spec.md:137)\n\n## Realistic release sizes\n\n| Scenario | Everything | Line only | Confidence and basis |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| **Eager restructuring, full behavior** | **94–97 KiB estimated** | **76–81 KiB estimated** | Medium-high. Type composition alone should leave everything near the current size. The combined CSS, bench, and shorter-hint scratch variants measured **95.16 / 77.42 KiB**. The range allows for real composition and packing overhead. |\n| **Lazy table, keyboard, and second renderer, initial download** | **89–93 KiB estimated** | **73–77 KiB estimated** | Medium. The actual Canvas and table DOM split with minified CSS measured **93.45 / 76.43 KiB** before the benchmark/text changes or deeper table and keyboard extraction. A combined cut with all three boundaries absent measured **88.12 / 72.17 KiB** before adding their loaders and eager shells. Keep an aggregate-download measurement as well. |\n| **Product reduced to fit 40/25 KiB** | **20.07 KiB measured** for a caller-prepared draw path | **15.81 KiB measured** for the corresponding line path | High confidence in the size of those scratch entries. They take a prepared `View` and layout inputs, and ship layout, SVG, HTML labels and minified CSS. The caller owns input processing, sizing, and the reader experience. They have no chart controller or live interaction. |\n\nThe old targets are **unsuitable for the required release scope**. A single combined cut that removed streaming, table and plot keyboard behavior, tooltip, zoom, key temporal paths, scatter picking, and Canvas still weighed **67.06 KiB everything / 54.96 KiB line**. Even a one-shot SVG scratch entry that took raw inputs and retained normalization, validation, axis fitting and CSS, while leaving out the chart controller and reader interactions, measured **45.41 / 38.80 KiB**. To ship at 40/25 with this architecture, the product would also have to narrow or delegate input handling and axis behavior. Requiring prepared numeric or category data, with application-owned temporal parsing, diagnostics and accessible readouts, is an example of a scope that fits. It gives up parts of F3–F4, A1–A4, DX2–DX5 and I6. [Release scope](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/02-product-spec.md:46) · [Pipeline and fidelity](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:169)\n\nPhase 1 measured **uPlot 23 KiB**, **D3 35 KiB**, **Chart.js 81 KiB**, **Nivo 129 KiB + React**, **Recharts 140 KiB + React**, **Highcharts 143 KiB with accessibility**, **MUI X 160 KiB + React**, **ApexCharts 289 KiB**, **ECharts 373 KiB**, and **Plotly 1,450 KiB**. These are the documented-import harness measurements, with CSS counted as text and React external where applicable. Phase 1 did not measure the smallest modular builds. [Phase 1 sizes and method](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/01-landscape.md:95)\n\nuPlot’s harness supplied dimensions, time units and a bar path, and got a pointer legend with no donut or data keyboard navigation. D3’s chart UI was authored for the harness. Chart.js covered the chart tasks and used a time adapter, but its default accessibility tree had no chart content. Highcharts with its accessibility module is the closest comparison for generated summaries and keyboard navigation. Recharts, ApexCharts and MUI X offered keyboard navigation in the tested configurations, with different focus and announcement behavior. No tested configuration offered a data table without a further module, and none gave the dirty-input field and row reporting Axylo requires. That makes 23 KiB a comparison for a narrower plotting product and 81–143 KiB a more useful context for Axylo’s proposed full gate. [Interaction and accessibility observations](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/01-landscape.md:250) · [Harness setup by library](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/01-landscape.md:308) · [Dirty input findings](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/01-landscape.md:7)\n\n## Work plan\n\n| Order and owner | Expected saving | Evidence required |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. **Core, data, view, scene, lookup, reader, render, distribution** · Compose each chart and separate `validate`. | **~18,000 B line**, estimated from the measured scratch delta. Budget approximately zero saving for everything from composition alone. | On packed ESM, assert that `line` excludes the point/donut stores and other types’ lookup and mark modules. Run typecheck, all five type-matrix cases, dense-line properties, strict create/update/append, packed Node `validate`, and all first-chart snippets. [Type matrix tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/type-matrix.browser.test.ts:42) · [Dense properties](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/acceptance/f-dense.test.ts:49) · [Packed Node tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/node.test.ts) |\n| 2. **Theme, build, docs** · Compile CSS once and update the default script snippet to request one copy. | **~1,600 B** per eager chart build. A separate **4,095 B** script-page saving at the current CSS size. | Compare packed pages with embedded and linked CSS under real CSP headers. Run forced-colors, dark-theme, text-spacing, reflow, and no-overlap cases. [CSP tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/csp.browser.test.ts:46) · [Renderer theme tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/render/theme.browser.test.ts:170) · [Reflow suite](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a6-a10.browser.test.ts) |\n| 3. **Core, bench, diagnostics, data** · Move instrumentation and consolidate exact duplicates. Shorten author advice after checking each affected code. | **~1,000–1,500 B estimated** across the selected changes, with overlap. | Run registry and delivery tests, production F12 in the figure and accessibility tree, strict rejection, and AX199 recovery. Check the benchmark against its existing readiness and revision assertions. [Registry tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/diagnostics/registry.test.ts) · [Production F12](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/acceptance/production.test.ts:19) · [Browser disclosure](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/f12-note.browser.test.ts:30) |\n| 4. **Render and distribution** · Rerun decision 0002, then split the alternate backend in ESM. | **~1,650 B everything / ~900 B line initially**, estimated from the split measurement. | Preserve SVG/Canvas pixel agreement and compare dense scatter, resize, theme, streaming and memory. Test the packed optional chunk and ordinary chart startup under CSP. [Pixel agreement](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/render/render.browser.test.ts:119) · [Renderer rerun requirements](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md:55) |\n| 5. **Reader, DOM, core** · Split the table presenter, then check whether the remaining table model is worth splitting. Keep keyboard eager unless the first-action tests pass under delayed loading. | **~900 B estimated** for the presenter. The whole table boundary has **2,923 B measured gross** available. Keyboard has **1,446 B measured gross** available. | Test first activation and focus return, jumping to a far row, a current zoom window, no dashboard reflow, keyboard entry and exit, and focus through streaming eviction. Repeat under delayed chunks and CSP. [Table acceptance](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a4-table.browser.test.ts:224) · [Keyboard acceptance](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a2-keyboard.browser.test.ts) · [Snapshot invariants](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/core/snapshot.test.ts:290) |\n| 6. **Release coordinator** · Measure the packed npm, IIFE and React cases and freeze DX7. | Proposed gates **96/80/2 KiB** eager, or **93/77 KiB** for initial npm downloads if the lazy split ships. | Run the full unit and browser suites, including the 494 Node acceptance cases in the cited run, and feed their results to the release checker. Record initial and all-chunk totals separately. [Acceptance runner](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/acceptance/README.md:3) · [Archived Node acceptance result](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core3.fix.report.md:96) |\n\nI made no repository edits and propose no changes to `public.ts` or `core/contracts.ts`. I ran the size experiments and in-memory Node smoke checks below. The browser behavior of the scratch variants, renderer performance, and the full acceptance suites remain unverified in this analysis. I treated DX7’s lazy scenario as a proposed initial-download metric, with eventual downloads accounted for separately.\n\n```text\nbaseline: dirty diagnostics/notes/table, zoom-follow append/keep and compact checks passed\nline scratch: dirty diagnostics/notes/table, zoom-follow append/keep and compact checks passed\nDOM-free production validate: dirty fixture codes passed\n```\n\n### Five largest practical wins\n\n| Win | Measured gzip saving | Applies to |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Compose and tree-shake `line` | **18,032 B** | Line-only ESM scratch, both renderers retained |\n| Request the stylesheet once | **4,095 B** at the current CSS size | Documented script-tag page |\n| Defer the table | **2,923 B gross** for the full boundary, **923 B net initially** for the tested presenter split | Everything, initial ESM |\n| Defer Canvas and keep SVG eager | **2,190 B gross** if omitted, **1,647 B net initially** with the tested split | Everything, initial ESM |\n| Minify the embedded stylesheet | **1,604 B** | Everything, eager production JS |","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
