{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/dist.fix.report.md; cat reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/acceptance-node-fix.report.md; cat reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/size.analysis.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' docs/02-product-spec.md; sed -n '220,470p' docs/02-product-spec.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Phase 1b: Axylo product specification\n\nRevision 3, 2026-11-18. Revised after three independent reviews ([ledger](../reviews/2026-11-18/ledger.md)), then updated with the owner's decisions of the same day: scatter is in 1.0, a compact variant is in 1.0, and the mark renderer is decided by measurement in M1.\n\nBased on the findings in [01-landscape.md](01-landscape.md). Finding IDs (R1–R16 for readers, D1–D10 for developers) refer to that document. Criterion IDs defined here are F, L, I, A (reader), DX (developer) and P (performance).\n\n## 1. What Axylo is\n\n**Axylo is the everyday chart you can ship without checking it.** The smallest correct call produces a chart that represents the data faithfully, stays readable at any width and in any color scheme, and works with a keyboard and a screen reader. When the data is wrong, Axylo says so, to the developer and to the reader, instead of drawing a guess.\n\nIt is a framework-neutral TypeScript library under the MIT license, with a thin React adapter.\n\nSpeed is a constraint, not the headline: Axylo must stay within the budgets in section 5 with default options. Whether it can do that at a million points with its accessibility layer on is something the first milestone measures, not something this document can promise.\n\n### 1.1 Why someone would choose it\n\nPhase 1 found no library among the ten that holds these together. Each row is the reason to choose Axylo over the library a team would otherwise reach for. The right-hand column is a design target, not a measured result.\n\n| If you would otherwise use | You get today | Axylo aims to add |\n|---|---|---|\n| Chart.js | Simple API, permissive license, widest reach | Keyboard and screen-reader access without author work; reduced-motion support; a tooltip that appears wherever the pointer is; large data without expert options; a stated time zone on every instant |\n| Recharts | The React default | A palette, a title, labels that fit, formatted numbers and dates without code; 100,000 points without seconds of blocking; the same chart outside React |\n| Highcharts | The best reader experience of the ten | The same class of accessibility with no module to import and no commercial license; reduced-motion support; errors that name the field and the row |\n| ECharts or ApexCharts | Many chart types | Fewer types, each of which fits its labels, reports bad data and is accessible by default |\n| uPlot | The speed reference | Automatic sizing, a palette, bars and donuts without path builders, and an accessibility layer, at a speed the budgets in section 5 keep honest |\n\nWhat Axylo does not try to win: number of chart types, visual customization depth, or exploratory analysis.\n\n### 1.2 Who it is for\n\nA developer adding charts to a product or an internal dashboard: data arrives as rows from an API, the chart is one component among many on the page, and the person writing it is not a visualization specialist and will not test it with a screen reader. Coding agents writing that code are the same audience: they benefit from typed fields, one logical data model and diagnostics that say what to change.\n\nNot the audience for 1.0: analysts exploring data in notebooks, designers building bespoke graphics, and teams that need maps, financial charts or network diagrams.\n\n### 1.3 Principles\n\n| Principle | In practice |\n|---|---|\n| Correct before configurable | A default that can mislead (truncated bars, interpolated gaps, a silently dropped label) is a bug, even if an option fixes it. |\n| Say it, do not guess | Anything Axylo cannot draw faithfully becomes a diagnostic with a code, a location and a fix. If values were left out, the reader is told too. |\n| The data is the authority | Exact values, the table, keyboard navigation and descriptions come from the normalized data. The drawing is derived from it and may be simplified; nothing else may be. |\n| Everything a reader needs is on; nothing else is | Title, units, legend, tooltip, keyboard, description and table are on. Entry animation, a marker on every point of a dense line, and decorative toolbars are not. |\n| One way to do each thing | One logical data model, one method per kind of change, one place for colors (CSS). |\n| The page is the theme | Font, text color and color scheme come from the page. |\n\n## 2. Scope of the first release\n\n### 2.1 Chart types\n\n| Type | Variants | Typical question | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Line | Multi-series, gaps; time, date, numeric or category x | How did it change? | 1.0 |\n| Bar | Vertical, horizontal, grouped, stacked | How do categories compare? | 1.0 |\n| Area | Single, stacked | How did the total and its parts change? | 1.0 |\n| Donut | Up to eight slices | What share does each part have? | 1.0 |\n| Scatter | Optional series | How are two measures related? | 1.0 (owner's decision) |\n| Compact variant | Of line, area and bar: marks only, for KPI tiles and sparklines | Which way is it going? | 1.0 (owner's decision) |\n\nPlus, on every cartesian chart: reference lines and bands (a target, an incident window).\n\nThe Phase 1 harness exercised line, bar and donut in all ten libraries (donut in nine; uPlot has none). Area and scatter are added on judgment; Phase 1 found no usage data by chart type. Two reviewers argued for fewer types and one for a compact variant; the owner decided on all five types plus the compact variant. Scatter alone needs two-dimensional hit testing and its own navigation model, so it is built last and carries the most schedule risk.\n\nEach type has its own rules for picking, navigation and limits. They are in the type matrix in section 3.6, and a type does not ship until its row is fully tested.\n\n### 2.2 Workflows\n\n| # | Workflow | What it requires of Axylo |\n|---|---|---|\n| W1 | Show a query result | Rows in, chart out; field types inferred where that is safe; bad rows reported. |\n| W2 | Fill a dashboard | Dozens of charts per page; container-sized; themed by the page; loading, empty and error states; a chart can take its name from the card's own heading. |\n| W3 | Compare categories | Long labels; horizontal bars; grouped and stacked. |\n| W4 | Watch a live metric | Append without redraw cost growing; a pause control; no announcement flood. |\n| W5 | Explore a range | Tooltip anywhere, legend toggle, zoom and pan by single click, drag, touch and keyboard. |\n| W6 | Read it another way | Keyboard, screen reader, table view, high contrast, reduced motion, 400% zoom, color-vision deficiency. |\n\n### 2.3 Supported environments\n\n| Dimension | 1.0 |\n|---|---|\n| Browsers | The current and previous major versions of Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari (macOS and iOS). |\n| TypeScript | 5.4 and later. Plain JavaScript is fully supported; types are an aid, not a requirement. |\n| React | 18 and 19, through `@axylo/react`. |\n| Text direction | Left-to-right pages. Right-to-left text renders, but the layout is not mirrored; this is stated in the conformance table. |\n| Built-in text | English, overridable (A10). |\n\n## 3. Acceptance criteria: the reader's experience\n\nEvery criterion maps to at least one test in the acceptance matrix kept in the repository (criterion → test → chart types it applies to → result). \"Property test\" means the statement is checked over at least 1,000 generated datasets. Expected values come from the fixture data and this specification, never from the implementation's output.\n\n### 3.1 Faithful representation\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| F1 | Bar and area value axes always include zero. An author `domain` that excludes zero is widened and raises AX110. | Property test on domains, including all-negative and mixed-sign data; `bars` fixture (values 82–97) starts at 0 | R1 |\n| F2 | Missing values are never drawn as zero and never bridged unless the author asks. An explicit `null` is a gap, silently. An isolated value between two gaps is a visible point. A missing bar shows a \"no data\" mark at the baseline, distinct from a zero bar. On a time or date axis with a regular step, an absent step is a gap and raises AX105; `missing: 'zero'` on the field turns absent and null values into zeros for counts. Tooltip, table and announcements say \"No data\". | `trend` with nulls and `trend` with the same 7 rows deleted render the same gap; `dirty` fixtures; property test that no segment connects across a missing index | R5 |\n| F3 | Input that cannot be plotted as given is reported, never guessed. The diagnostic registry (section 3.5) fixes each case's level, what is drawn and what the reader sees. | One test per code; the combined `dirty` fixture and the four one-bad-value fixtures yield exactly the codes listed in section 3.5 | R5, D5 |\n| F4 | Three temporal types, never mixed silently. **Calendar dates** (`2026-02-24`, `2026-02`, `2026`) read the same in every viewer time zone. **Instants** (epoch milliseconds declared `type: 'time'`, `Date` objects, ISO strings with an offset) are shown in `timeZone` (default: the viewer's), with the zone named in the tooltip heading and the description. **Wall-clock times** (date-time strings without an offset) are shown as written, with hint AX112. Instants that all fall on the same time of day, whole days apart, raise hint AX111 (\"these look like calendar dates\"). A bare number is never assumed to be an instant. | Four fixture variants in UTC, America/Los_Angeles and Asia/Kolkata: (a) date strings: identical text in all three; (b) ISO strings with `Z`, (c) epoch ms with `type: 'time'`, (d) `Date` objects: each zone's correct local text, zone visible, AX111 raised; with `timeZone: 'UTC'` or `type: 'date'`: identical. Plus: both DST transitions, a leap day, an unparseable date | R3 |\n| F5 | No smoothing. When a line is denser than the screen, the drawing is simplified with this guarantee: for every device-pixel column, and every contiguous run of valid samples inside it, the drawn stroke covers exactly that run's minimum and maximum. A break between two runs is always kept, even when both fall in one pixel column; a slot a series merely has no row for (weekly data beside daily) does not break its line. Stacked areas are simplified on their cumulative sums. Exact values always come from the data, not the drawing. | Assertions on which source samples the drawing kept, for: one outlier in 1,000,000; two peaks and a gap in one column; alternating valid and missing samples; tied extremes; device pixel ratio 1 and 2; several zoom levels. A sample that the drawing dropped is still reachable by keyboard and in the table | D8, D9 |\n| F6 | Numbers and dates are formatted for the page locale. Ticks and readouts are formatted separately: ticks may be compact (`50K`); tooltip, table and announcements show the full integer part, and the fraction with up to six significant digits not counting the fraction's leading zeros (`123,456.75`; `0.000123456`; `0.1 + 0.2` reads `0.3`), with the unit. A custom formatter that throws falls back to the default and raises a diagnostic. | Expected strings for chosen values (large integers, small decimals, percentages, zoned times) in `en-US`, `de-DE` and `en-IN` | R4 |\n| F7 | Domains follow one order of precedence: the zoomed view is inside the author's `domain` if given, otherwise inside the automatic domain. The automatic domain contains all data, reference lines and stack totals, and ends at the first tick at or beyond each extreme. A field declared `scale: 'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1) ends at full scale only when its maximum is at least half of full scale and not above it. Nothing is inferred from a label. | Property tests; `bars` ends at 100 when declared `percent`; a 4% maximum ends at the first tick at or above 4% | R1 |\n| F8 | Donut: slices keep the given order, start at 12 o'clock and run clockwise. The legend shows every slice's value and percentage as text, in slice order. A zero slice is listed without an arc; a `null` slice is listed as \"No data\". A negative value is AX120 and the chart shows its error state. With more than eight slices that have an arc (a positive value), the seven largest are drawn and the rest of those are grouped as \"Other (n)\", with AX121; zero and `null` slices never count toward the limit and are never grouped, so each stays listed on its own; the table lists every category. Shares are shown with at most one fraction digit. An all-zero donut shows the empty state. | `parts` fixture; one test per case | R7, landscape scoreboard 3.1 |\n| F9 | Up to eight series, no two share a color. Beyond eight, each series still has a unique combination of color and dash or marker (for bars: color and fill pattern, which gives 32 distinct combinations; beyond 32 bar series combinations repeat), and AX130 is raised. | Tests with 6, 8 and 12 series | R7 |\n| F10 | Stacks place positive and negative values on their own sides of zero, and the axis covers both totals. The readout's total is the sum when every component has the same sign; when signs are mixed it gives the positive total and the negative total, the two ends the reader sees, not their net. A missing component is drawn with zero height and the stack is marked \"incomplete\" in tooltip, table and announcements. | Property test on stacked geometry; mixed-sign and incomplete fixtures | R5 |\n| F11 | Line, area and scatter are drawn in x order whatever the input order. On line, area and bar, two rows with the same x in one series are AX108 and that series is not drawn. On scatter, points sharing an x (or both coordinates) are ordinary data. | Shuffled `trend` renders identically; duplicate fixture | R5 |\n| F12 | When a warning means values were left out or changed, the chart says so to the reader: a short note (\"Values not shown: 2. See table.\", worded so it reads correctly for any count) inside the figure and in the accessibility tree, in every build. | Production build given `dirty` shows the note | R5, R6 |\n\n### 3.2 Legibility\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L1 | No visible text overlaps other text or is clipped, and no label disappears without the reader being told. Remedies, in order: thin (time, date, numeric and `ordered` category axes only); wrap to at most three lines; rotate up to 45°; truncate in the middle, keeping every label distinct, with the full text in tooltip and table. If labels still do not fit, a visible note (\"Some labels hidden. See table.\") appears and AX143 suggests horizontal bars. Axylo never changes a chart's orientation on its own. | In real browsers, after fonts load: for widths 240–1,920 px and generated label sets, zero intersecting text boxes (1 px tolerance), zero text outside the figure, truncated labels pairwise distinct. Repeated with the WCAG 1.4.12 text-spacing overrides, 200% text-only zoom, and a font that loads late | R2 |\n| L2 | Text is at least 12 CSS px and at least 4.5:1 against what is behind it, including text drawn inside a mark and legend entries for hidden series. Marks, axis lines and focus indicators are at least 3:1. Gridlines are exempt: they are not needed to read a value while the tooltip and table exist. Holds in light and dark. | Computed-style and rendered-pixel checks in both schemes | R7, R10 |\n| L3 | The chart follows its container with no call from the page, in both directions. With no definite height, the plot is 16:9, clamped between 160 and 480 CSS px tall, and the title, controls and table flow above and below it. A container that starts hidden or zero-sized draws when it gets a size. | Resize 720 → 360 → 720 px; hidden-then-shown test; `height: auto` container is stable over 10 frames | R16, D1 |\n| L4 | Light or dark is taken from the chart element's computed `color-scheme`; if that is not set, from the luminance of the inherited text color. `--axylo-background` overrides both. Marks repaint within one frame when the scheme, forced colors, contrast preference, or the `class`, `style` or `data-theme` of `<html>` or `<body>` changes. Forced-colors mode uses system colors. | A page that sets `color-scheme`; a class-toggled dark theme without it; a light-only page on a dark device (must stay light); a runtime toggle; forced-colors emulation | R10 |\n| L5 | A marker on every point appears only when adjacent points are at least 12 px apart (provisional value), and always for isolated points. | `trend` at 720 px has none; a 12-point line has them | R8 |\n| L6 | Title, subtitle and axis titles with units are part of the chart. A value axis is titled with its label (the field name when none is given) and unit. An x axis that is a measure (number) is titled the same way; a time, date or category x axis is titled only when the author gives a `label`, since its ticks already say what it is. A reference line or band shows its label next to it. Axis titles never overlap tick labels. | Layout tests; `trend` fixture | R9 |\n| L7 | Empty data shows a message inside the plot area; so do loading, error and Axylo's own internal failures. No invented axis ranges, no blank box. A change of state is announced once, politely. | `empty` fixture: message visible and in the accessibility tree, no tick labels; forced internal error shows the error state | R6 |\n| L8 | When the figure cannot hold a readable plot (less than 120×80 CSS px for the plot), it shows the title, a short summary (the first sentence of the generated summary, on at most two lines, cut with an ellipsis beyond that; the full summary remains the figure's description) and the table control instead, and raises AX142. | Narrow and short container tests | R2 |\n\n### 3.3 Interaction\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| I1 | Pointing anywhere in the plot shows what is there, by the picking rule for the chart type (section 3.6), within one frame at the 95th percentile. On line, area and bar the tooltip lists every visible series at the nearest x; a series with no row at that x reads \"No observation\", distinct from \"No data\". The tooltip stays inside the viewport, does not cover the active mark or any control, can itself be hovered, and lists at most eight rows plus \"+N more\". In a plot too small for it (decision 0004: narrower than 240 px or shorter than 140 px) the same content appears as text in a strip reserved under the plot. Escape closes it until the pointer reaches a different datum. | Hover at 20 positions including all four edges; sparse-series fixture; narrow tile with eight series; Escape test | R15 |\n| I2 | Legend entries are toggle buttons with a pressed state, operable by click, Enter and Space. | Keyboard and accessibility-tree tests | R12 |\n| I3 | Zoom on time, date and numeric x axes is reachable four ways: visible Zoom in, Zoom out and Reset buttons that work with single clicks; drag to select a range; `+`, `-`, `0` and Shift+arrows; and pinch when `zoom: 'touch'` is set. The wheel zooms only while the plot has focus, and never when Ctrl or Meta is held, so browser zoom and page scrolling are not intercepted. | Reach a given range by clicks only; by keyboard only; by drag; page scrolls normally with the pointer over an unfocused chart | D7, R12, WCAG 2.5.1, 2.5.7, 1.4.4 |\n| I4 | Every interaction works with a single pointer without dragging, and by touch. Every control is at least 24×24 CSS px. A one-finger drag on a chart scrolls the page. | Touch emulation; size checks | WCAG 2.5.7, 2.5.8 |\n| I5 | First render is not animated. An update may transition for at most 200 ms. Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` nothing transitions, on first render or on update. | After `document.fonts.ready`: first frame equals the frame at 2.5 s within a 0.1% pixel tolerance, in both settings; an update under reduced motion reaches its final frame in one step; a 24-chart filter change settles within 200 ms | R14 |\n| I6 | A chart fed by `append` shows a Pause/Resume control. While paused, new rows are buffered and the view does not move. | Stream test | WCAG 2.2.2 |\n\n### 3.4 Accessibility\n\nTarget: every WCAG 2.2 level AA success criterion that applies to what Axylo renders, as component-level evidence. Conformance of a page is a property of the page and remains the author's.\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A1 | Every chart has an accessible name: its `title`, or the heading named by `labelledBy`, or else a generated name (\"Line chart of Sessions by Day\") with AX140 raised in every build. The title's heading level is the author's (`level`), and it can be hidden visually. Every chart has a generated summary: type, series, x range with time zone, y range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value, and any omissions. `description` adds to the summary and never removes the zone or omission statements. Element ids are unique per chart. | Accessibility-tree snapshot for every fixture; a 24-chart page has no duplicate ids; axe `heading-order` | R11, R13 |\n| A2 | Keyboard: at most two tab stops while the table is closed, the plot and one toolbar with roving focus that holds legend toggles, zoom, pause and the table control. Every point is reachable in order; the series can be switched; the ends can be reached directly; focus can leave the chart. The focus indicator is at least 2 px and 3:1 and is not hidden by the tooltip. The exact keys and roles are fixed after the pattern spike in M1 (section 8). | Forward and reverse traversal in collapsed, zoomed, streaming and table-open states; focus after hiding the focused series; indicator contrast | R12 |\n| A3 | Moving focus to a point conveys its text (series, x, value with unit, position) through the focused element's accessible name or a polite live region. On touch screen readers, every point is reachable by swiping, or the table is reachable within two gestures. | Accessibility-tree snapshot after each key; a recorded rehearsal on NVDA with Chrome, NVDA with Firefox, VoiceOver with Safari on macOS, VoiceOver on iOS, and TalkBack with Chrome | R12, R13 |\n| A4 | \"View as table\" shows the data as an HTML table built from the normalized data when opened: the title as caption, field labels with units as column headers, x values as row headers, values formatted as in F6. It covers the current x range, 100 rows per page, with every row reachable by paging or by jumping to an x value. It sits in a focusable scroll region no taller than the plot, and opening it does not move other charts. | Table content compared with fixture data, including a value near row 900,000 of 1,000,000; a 24-chart page layout test | R13 |\n| A5 | Series are never distinguished by color alone, in any mode. Each line and area carries a shape marker at its last point and at sparse intervals, repeated in the legend and tooltip; under `prefers-contrast: more` or forced colors, lines also differ by dash. Adjacent filled marks (slices, stacked segments) are separated by at least 1 px of background. The default palette has eight colors, each at least 3:1 on the default light and dark backgrounds, with a minimum pairwise OKLab difference of 0.10 (provisional) under normal vision and simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia. On any other background, Axylo measures the palette against the resolved background and raises AX141 below 3:1. | Palette script scoring the rendered marks of an eight-series chart in both schemes, repeats included; rendered-pixel checks of separators; a grayscale screenshot in which three crossing lines can be told apart by marker | R7, WCAG 1.4.1, 1.4.11 |\n| A6 | Content reflows at 320 CSS px and 400% zoom without two-dimensional scrolling. User text-spacing overrides do not clip text. | Viewport and style-override tests in three engines | WCAG 1.4.10, 1.4.12 |\n| A7 | Data arriving does not speak. Announcements happen only for the reader's own navigation, and for a change of state (L7). While data streams, keyboard focus stays on the same datum until it leaves the window. | Stream test counting live-region changes with and without focus; 24 concurrent streams | R12 |\n| A8 | axe-core reports no violations on any fixture in any state, including `heading-order` and `scrollable-region-focusable`. | CI | R11 |\n| A9 | A conformance table lists every applicable WCAG 2.2 AA success criterion, the test that covers it, and what the author must supply (a meaningful title or `labelledBy`, series names, units, the page's own structure). It also states the limits in section 3.8. | Published with each release; a CI check that every listed test exists | R11 |\n| A10 | Every built-in string can be replaced through `messages`. Built-in English text is marked `lang=\"en\"` when the document language is not English. | Test on a `lang=\"de\"` page | WCAG 3.1.2 |\n\n### 3.5 Diagnostic registry\n\nLevel decides delivery. **Error**: the chart or a series cannot be drawn; the chart shows its error state. **Warning**: something is not drawn as given; the reader sees the note in F12. **Hint**: advice for the author. Errors and warnings print once per code per page in every build; hints print in development builds only. `strict: true` throws on errors and warnings found while validating a create, `update` or `append` call, synchronously from that call. A diagnostic that can only arise later (a contrast check after the page's CSS changes, a custom formatter that throws when a table page is built, a label that stops fitting after a resize) is reported when it is found and is never thrown. Every diagnostic names the chart; those about data name the field and the first row indexes in the caller's own data.\n\n| Code | Level | Condition | What is drawn |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| AX100 | Error | Typed columns have different lengths | Error state; the message names the columns and their lengths |\n| AX101 | Error | A named field is not a key of any row | Error state; the message lists the keys that exist and the closest match |\n| AX102 | Warning | A field is absent (`undefined`) in some rows | Gaps |\n| AX103 | Warning | A value is `NaN` or infinite | Gap |\n| AX104 | Hint | Every non-missing value of a value field is a plain decimal string (`'40'`, `'-1.5'`) | Parsed and drawn; the message suggests `parse: 'number'`. An empty string counts as missing |\n| AX105 | Hint | Steps are absent on a regular time, date, wall-clock or number axis | Gaps. When a series would need more absent steps than twice its observations (and more than 1,000), it is treated as irregular instead: no gaps are created and the hint says so |\n| AX106 | Warning | A value field mixes numbers with strings, or holds strings that are not plain decimals (`'1,234'`, `'n/a'`) | The strings are not drawn |\n| AX107 | Warning | A date or time cannot be parsed | That row is not drawn |\n| AX108 | Error | Duplicate x within a series, or duplicate donut labels | That series, or the donut, is not drawn |\n| AX109 | Hint | Numbers on an axis declared `time` look like seconds, or a numeric x looks like epoch milliseconds without `type: 'time'` | As declared |\n| AX110 | Warning | An author domain on bar or area excluded zero | Domain widened to zero |\n| AX111 | Hint | Instants look like calendar dates | As instants, zone shown |\n| AX112 | Hint | Date-time strings have no offset | As written |\n| AX120 | Error | Negative donut value | Error state |\n| AX121 | Warning | More than eight donut slices | Seven largest plus \"Other (n)\" |\n| AX130 | Warning | More than eight series | All series, with dash or marker differences |\n| AX140 | Warning | No `title` and no `labelledBy` | Generated name |\n| AX141 | Warning | Palette below 3:1 on the resolved background | As themed |\n| AX142 | Hint | Plot too small to draw | Summary and table control |\n| AX143 | Hint | Labels hidden after every remedy | Note shown to the reader |\n| AX150 | Warning | Unknown option key | Ignored; the message suggests the closest known key |\n| AX151 | Warning | `append` rows out of order | Accepted through the slower full update |\n| AX131 | Hint | A compact chart has more than one series | Drawn, with dash differences |\n| AX132 | Hint | More bars than pixels along the category axis | Drawn; the message suggests aggregating |\n| AX133 | Hint | A scatter chart has more than 50,000 points | Drawn; the message says drawing may be slow |\n| AX134 | Error | Series × x positions exceeds 20,000,000 (long rows with many series and little shared x) | Error state; the message gives both counts and suggests fewer series or a scatter chart |\n| AX152 | Warning | `timeZone` is not a zone the browser knows | The viewer's zone is used |\n| AX160 | Warning | A custom formatter threw | The default format is used for that value |\n| AX199 | Error | Axylo itself failed while building the chart | Error state with a generic message; the error is passed to `onDiagnostic` and printed |\n\nExpected results for the Phase 1 fixtures: the combined `dirty` fixture (`[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]`) yields AX102, AX103 and AX106 once each, draws Mon–Tue as a segment and Sun as a point, and shows the F12 note. `dirty-string` (one `'40'` among numbers) yields AX106. A column made only of numeric strings yields AX104 and is drawn in full. `dirty-null` yields nothing.\n\nThe split between AX104 and AX106 answers a reviewer's objection that common back ends return counts as strings (Postgres `bigint` and `numeric`, BigQuery): a whole column of plain decimal strings is unambiguous and is parsed; a stray string among numbers is a bug and is not.\n\n### 3.6 Type matrix\n\n| | Line | Area | Bar | Donut | Scatter |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Picking by pointer | Nearest x | Nearest x | The band under the pointer | The slice under the pointer | Nearest point within 24 px; ties go to the later row |\n| Tooltip content | Every visible series at that x | Same, plus the stack total | Every series in that category | Label, value, percentage | The point's series, x and y; coincident points listed, up to eight |\n| Keyboard order | By x, then series | By x, then series | By category, then series | By slice order | By x, then y |\n| Zoom | On time, date and numeric x only | On time, date and numeric x only | On time, date and numeric x only | No | Yes, both axes later; x only in 1.0 |\n| Simplified drawing when dense | F5 | F5 on cumulative sums | No; more bars than pixels raises a hint | No | No in 1.0; above 50,000 points a hint says drawing may be slow |\n| Limit that raises a diagnostic | Eight series | Eight series | Eight series | Eight slices | Eight series |\n| Reference lines and bands | Yes; included in the domain; listed in the summary and table caption | Yes | Yes | No | Yes |\n\n### 3.7 Compact variant\n\n`compact: true` on line, area and bar draws the marks and nothing else: no axes, gridlines, legend, toolbar, table control, zoom or tooltip. It is for a small tile where the page already shows the number and the label.\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by |\n|---|---|---|\n| C1 | A compact chart is a static image with a full text alternative: `role=\"img\"`, an accessible name (as in A1, including AX140 when none is given) and the generated summary as its description (series, x range, minimum, maximum, last value, omissions). It has no tab stop and no pointer-only behavior, so nothing is available to a mouse that is not available to a keyboard. | Accessibility-tree snapshot; a page of 40 has zero added tab stops |\n| C2 | The fidelity rules still hold: zero baseline for bar and area (F1), gaps (F2), diagnostics (F3), simplified drawing (F5), sorting (F11), no motion (I5), scheme from the page (L4). | The same fixtures as the full charts |\n| C3 | It fills its container at any size from 24×16 CSS px up; with no definite height it is 32 px tall. | Size tests |\n| C4 | With more than one series, lines differ by dash as well as color, since there is no legend; AX131 suggests one series per compact chart. | Two-series fixture |\n| C5 | When values were left out (F12), the description says so and a small marker with a text equivalent appears in the corner. | `dirty` fixture in compact mode |\n\nA compact chart does not give access to every value; the summary is its alternative. An author who needs every value reachable uses the full chart.\n\n### 3.8 What the evidence will and will not show\n\nAutomated checks establish structure: names, roles, focus order, contrast, text. They do not establish that a screen-reader user finds a chart understandable. The only evidence for that in 1.0 is A3's rehearsal by the developer on five reader and browser pairs, recorded as rehearsal. Axylo is built by one person with no access to study participants, so no claim of usability testing with disabled users is made, and the conformance table says so in those words. Reviewers proposed participant testing as a release gate; it is recorded here as a known limit instead.\n\n## 4. Acceptance criteria: the developer's experience\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| DX1 | The first chart takes ten lines or fewer, including import, by npm, by script tag and in React. No required option besides data and fields. | Three CI tests that render the documented snippet from the packed distribution | D1 |\n| DX2 | One logical table, three ways to pass it: long rows, wide rows, typed columns. The three forms of each fixture produce the same normalized data and the same diagnostics, with row indexes in the caller's own form. | Equivalence tests | D2 |\n| DX3 | With typed rows, a misspelled field, a text field on a value axis, wide `y` combined with `series`, and an option that does not apply to the chart type all fail to compile. At run time, for untyped rows, the same mistakes raise AX101, AX106 and AX150. | [docs/api/examples.ts](api/examples.ts) compiles and each marked mistake fails (already checked with TypeScript 7.0.2); runtime tests per code | D5 |\n| DX4 | Diagnostics follow the registry in section 3.5: stable code, chart name, field and rows where they apply, the fix, and a documentation page per code. | Message snapshots; link checker | R5, D5 |\n| DX5 | Three methods change a chart: `update` changes options, `append` adds rows, `zoom` sets the view. They apply in call order. Validation and diagnostics are synchronous; drawing is deferred to the next frame; marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer always switch to a new state together. `destroy()` releases everything and cancels pending work. No `resize()` exists. | Sequence tests: replace then append then zoom in one turn; a rejected `strict` update leaves the last valid chart; destroy before the frame; 1,000 create–destroy cycles show no heap growth trend (P7) | D3, R16 |\n| DX6 | Colors, font and spacing are CSS custom properties with documented names; the chart inherits `font-family` and `color`. `colors` pins a color to a series name so the same series has the same color on every chart. There is no theme object and no positional palette option. | Theming tests, including a 24-tile page where series subsets differ | R10 |\n| DX7 | Zero runtime dependencies (reconsidered only with a measured comparison). Size targets, provisional until M1 measures a working slice: 40 KiB gzipped for everything, 25 KiB for a page that uses only `line`, 2 KiB for the React adapter, CSS included. | size-limit in CI on the packed distribution | D4 |\n| DX8 | Importing in Node without a DOM does not throw, and `validate()` runs there. Works under React StrictMode, including setup, cleanup and setup again. Works under a Content Security Policy without `unsafe-inline` or `unsafe-eval`, served with real headers. Labels and formatter output are inserted as text, never as HTML. A target that matches no element throws an error that names the selector. | CI tests | D1 |\n| DX9 | Semantic versioning. A deprecated API keeps working for at least one minor release and six months. At most one major version a year. The previous release's documented examples still compile and run against each release candidate. | Release policy; API report diff; upgrade test | D6 |\n| DX10 | Every runnable example in the documentation is compiled and rendered in CI from the packed distribution. | Docs build | D1 |\n| DX11 | MIT license for everything, including zoom and accessibility. Third-party notices are verified at release. | Repository; release check | D7 |\n| DX12 | A coding agent gets the first chart right. Given fixed prompts and trap fixtures (UTC-midnight ISO dates, numeric strings, unsorted rows, missing days, a misspelled field), an agent with only the published docs produces a chart with no error diagnostics that passes the reader criteria. Recorded as agent rehearsal, not as user evidence. | Scripted rehearsal at each release | D1, 1.2 |\n\n## 5. Performance budgets\n\nThese numbers are provisional. They are design targets taken from Phase 1's order-of-magnitude measurements, and they become release gates only after the benchmark harness built in M0 has measured a working Axylo slice in M1 and the limits have been recalibrated and frozen (plan, M1 and M6). Each budget names its workload, metric and statistic so that two people measuring the same build get the same verdict.\n\n| ID | Workload | Metric | Statistic | Provisional limit | Phase 1 reference |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| P1 | `trend` (3×120), default options, reference desktop | Main-thread task time from the call until the chart is complete | Median of 20 runs | 10 ms | Not comparable across libraries below about 35 ms with the Phase 1 method |\n| P2 | One series, 100,000 and 1,000,000 points, typed columns | Same | Median of 20 | 60 ms and 250 ms | See 01, section 5.3: uPlot and tuned Chart.js are the fastest measured |\n| P3 | 600-point window, one appended point per display frame at 60 Hz, driven by an independent clock | Main-thread task time per update (per run: task time in the window divided by the number of updates); and frames later than 25 ms | Median over runs; count per 300 frames | 2 ms; 0 | See 01, section 5.3 |\n| P4 | Pointer moved across a 100,000-point chart | Time from input to the frame showing the matching readout | 95th percentile | One frame (16.7 ms at 60 Hz) | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n| P5 | 24 `trend` charts created on one page, each tile 340 × 200 px | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 200 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n| P6 | Download | Minified and gzipped bytes, CSS included | Exact | DX7 | 01, section 1.4 |\n| P8 | 40 compact charts created on one page, each 160 × 48 px with one series | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 80 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n| P7 | Memory | Bytes owned by Axylo beyond the caller's columns for 1,000,000 points; heap trend over 1,000 create–destroy cycles | Measured after forced collection | At most 1× the size of the caller's two columns; no upward slope | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n\nThree rules hold whatever the numbers become:\n\n1. Budgets apply to default options. Finding D8 showed libraries that are fast only for authors who know which options to set.\n2. Budgets are absolute, on named hardware profiles. Comparison libraries, uPlot above all, are measured in the same runs and published beside Axylo, but a ratio to another library is not a gate: it would reward leaving out work that library does not do.\n3. The accessibility layer is on in every measured configuration.\n\n## 6. Decisions and the findings behind them\n\n\"Status\" records what the reviews changed.\n\n| Decision | What decided it | Status after review |\n|---|---|---|\n| Diagnostics are a first-class feature with codes | R5: none of the ten named the field, the row or the problem. | Kept. Severity and delivery rewritten (3.5): readers are told too, and production builds are not silent. |\n| Calendar dates, instants and wall-clock times are different types | R3: given instants, six libraries show a daily value on a different day in some zones, which is correct for instants and wrong for dates; given date strings, most of them get it right. The gap is that nothing says which one the author meant. | Kept, sharpened. The test now separates the types, and a hint fires when instants look like dates. Default zone for instants: the viewer's, with the zone always visible. |\n| Accessibility is in the core, on by default | R11–R13. | Kept. The table is built on demand, not eagerly. |\n| The normalized data, not the drawing, is the source for values, table, keyboard and summary | Reviews: a simplified drawing cannot return the values it dropped. | New. |\n| No animation on first render | Reading starts at first paint, and with 24 charts loading at once motion competes with it. R14: four of seven animating libraries ignore reduced motion. | Kept, with the rationale restated. Update transitions stay capped at 200 ms. |\n| Label fitting is a tested invariant with a visible fallback | R2. | Changed. The absolute \"everything always fits\" was infeasible in a 240 px tile; it is now \"nothing overlaps, and nothing disappears without the reader being told\". Rotation comes before truncation. |\n| One logical table, three input forms | D2. | Changed from \"one data shape\": typed columns and wide rows are first-class. |\n| Three methods with a commit contract | D3. | Changed from \"one update method\": `append` and `zoom` were always there, and their ordering is now specified. |\n| Canvas marks with DOM text | D9: a canvas library is the speed reference. D10: one DOM node per point is slow. | **Reopened.** Phase 1 does not show that SVG is slow, only that one node per point is; a single SVG path per series was fast. M1 builds both mark renderers on the same scene and measures them (section 8). Canvas is the working assumption. |\n| Simplified drawing that preserves extremes, on by default | D8. | Kept for lines and areas, with the guarantee restated per pixel column and per run of valid samples. \"Every spike survives\" was wrong as written. |\n| Palette held to measurable thresholds | R7. | Kept as a screen, marked provisional. Marks carry a non-color cue in every mode, which the first draft did not require. |\n| Colors and fonts from CSS | R10. | Kept. Repaint triggers specified; `palette` option replaced by `colors` keyed by series name. |\n| Numeric strings | R5. | Changed: a whole column of plain decimal strings is parsed with a hint; mixed columns are not. |\n| Chart types | Judgment, then the owner's decision. | All five in 1.0, plus the compact variant (3.7). |\n| MIT, including zoom and accessibility | D7. | Kept. |\n| React adapter in 1.0 | Recharts leads npm installs among chart-level libraries. | Kept, and built early to test the core API. Downloads show where charts are written, not that Axylo will be wanted there. |\n| Budgets apply to defaults | D8. | Kept. The ratio to uPlot is no longer a gate. |\n| Zero runtime dependencies | D4. | Kept provisionally; time-zone arithmetic uses `Intl`. |\n\n## 7. Changes this revision rejected\n\n| Proposal | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| Test with screen-reader users, outside developers or design partners before release | One-person project with no participants. Recorded as a stated limit (3.8). Agent rehearsal (DX12) and developer rehearsal (A3) are used and labelled as such. |\n| Collect chart sizes and device profiles from beta deployments | A chart library should not phone home. Workload sizes come from the spec and from issue reports. |\n| Switch to horizontal bars automatically when labels do not fit | Changing orientation changes what the chart says at a glance; Axylo suggests it and tells the reader what is hidden. |\n| Remove the first-chart line-count criterion | Kept as a floor, and joined by DX12, which tests getting it right. |\n\n## 8. Decisions still open\n\nSettled by the owner on 2026-11-18: scatter is in 1.0; the compact variant is in 1.0; the mark renderer stays open until M1 measures both.\n\n| Question | Recommendation | Settled by |\n|---|---|---|\n| Canvas or SVG paths for marks? | Decide from the M1 comparison: P1, P2, P3, P5, bytes, memory, theme toggle, forced colors, print. | M1 record. |\n| Which keyboard and screen-reader pattern for the plot? | Decide from the M1 spike: three candidate patterns on the `trend` chart, rehearsed on the five pairs. | M1 record, confirmed in M4. |\n| Is the palette that meets A5 one people want to look at? | Hand-tune from the search output with the thresholds in CI. | M5. |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/dist.fix.report.md; cat reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/acceptance-node-fix.report.md; cat reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/size.analysis.md'","aggregated_output":"<!-- Author: Claude Opus 5.5 (requested effort high), Agent-tool subagent a6f0d7846bf125638, resumed. Saved by the coordinator from the hand-back. -->\n\n## Fix pass on the distribution tests (review findings 2, 3, 20–24)\n\nAll seven findings are addressed and every distribution test passes. Finding 2 is a real product defect, now fixed with a two-specifier edit in `packages/axylo/src/index.ts`. Nothing is committed.\n\n### Final runs\nAgainst HEAD `ba11e6f` plus the other agents' uncommitted core changes, with dist rebuilt by `node scripts/pack.mjs` at about 07:25 CET. Final runs were at 07:42–07:47 CET.\n\n- **Node**, `npx vitest run --config tests/dist/vitest.config.ts`: `Test Files 9 passed (9)` / `Tests 106 passed (106)`.\n- **Browser**, `AXYLO_BROWSER=1 npx vitest run --config tests/dist/vitest.config.ts`: `Test Files 4 passed (4)` / `Tests 30 passed (30)` / `Duration 36.27s`.\n- **`npm run typecheck`**: all four projects pass (`tsc -p packages/axylo --noEmit && tsc -p docs/api && tsc -p packages/react && tsc -p tests/dist`), exit 0.\n- **`npm test`**: 11 files fail (77 tests), all in other agents' work: modified `acceptance/f-domains`, `f-donut` and `f-stacks`, plus `acceptance/.scratch/*`, which look like deliberately broken variants. Every failure is a behaviour assertion such as `expected [] to deeply equal ['AX121']`, never an import or resolution error. The three unit test files that import the changed entry, plus `packages/react/test`, pass: 5 files, 139 tests.\n\n### Per finding\n\n**2. NodeNext consumers lose the public types (blocking).**\n- Test first: added `fixtures/npm/tsconfig.nodenext.json` and `fixtures/react/tsconfig.nodenext.json`, and `types.test.ts` now runs both fixtures under Bundler and NodeNext.\n- Before the fix, both NodeNext runs failed with TS2834 at `node_modules/axylo/dist/index.d.ts` lines 5 and 6, TS2459 (types not exported), and unused `@ts-expect-error`. The React fixture also failed in the adapter's own `index.d.ts`.\n- The fix (re-read the file right before editing; nothing else touched):\n  - `packages/axylo/src/index.ts` line 7: `} from './public';` → `} from './public.js';`\n  - line 12: `export type * from './public';` → `export type * from './public.js';`\n- **Not changed:** `packages/react/src/index.ts`, because its emitted `index.d.ts` has no relative specifiers; the React NodeNext run passes once the core's declarations are fixed. The `./core/*` runtime imports in `index.ts` are also unchanged, since they never reach the declarations. No build configs were changed.\n- After the rebuild, `dist/index.d.ts` carries `./public.js`, and all 12 type tests pass. The new specifier still resolves in the workspace type check, in Vitest (the tests above) and in esbuild (`npm run build`).\n\n**3. Size check compared rounded KiB.**\n- `scripts/size.mjs` now exports `exceeds(bytes, targetKiB)`, which compares integer bytes against `targetKiB * 1024`.\n- The measuring code only runs when the script is called directly, so the function can be imported.\n- The header says `--check` is correct but not a CI gate while the targets are under review. I did not change CI.\n- New `tests/dist/size.test.ts` covers the boundary, e.g. 40,961 B against 40 KiB is over.\n- Current sizes:\n\n| Bundle | gzip | Target | `--check` |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| line only | 99.38 KiB | 25 KiB | over |\n| everything | 99.47 KiB | 40 KiB | over |\n| script tag (JS + CSS) | 103.53 KiB | none | — |\n| React adapter | 1.17 KiB | 2 KiB | passes |\n\n`--check` exits 1.\n\n**20. Text insertion.** New `text.browser.test.ts`, served under the strict policy. It runs from the minified script-tag file and from a bundled module under the development condition.\n- **Sentinels:** `pages/sentinels.ts` gives every text source on each of the five chart types its own inert sentinel, `<u data-axs=\"id\">…</u>`:\n  - title, subtitle, description, series name and category;\n  - x and y field labels, unit;\n  - x and y value formatters, and tick formatters;\n  - a compact chart.\n- **Exact assertions:** heading text and accessible name are compared exactly; ticks must match exactly their formatter's output for a number; legend entries are compared exactly.\n- **Containment assertions:** the description, axis titles, tooltip/readout parts and `aria-valuetext` must each contain the right sentinels.\n- **Table:** the caption must equal the title sentinel exactly. Headers and every cell are compared against the fixture row they came from.\n- **Page-wide checks:** every `data-axs` anywhere in the page text or attributes must belong to a whole sentinel, no `[data-axs]` or `u` element may exist, and the observer must have recorded nothing.\n- **Messages:**\n  - A second page overrides every `Messages` key and drives 13 chart states (dirty data, zoomed, untitled, time zone, references, loading, empty, error, ten series, eleven slices, a sparse stack, and the dirty-input note).\n  - It requires 36 named keys, listed by the surface the spec gives each.\n  - The dirty-input note must read exactly ``Values not shown: 3. ${S('m-seeTable')}``, and author error text must appear as text.\n  - Keys that no triggered state shows are listed in `tests/dist/README.md`.\n\n**21. StrictMode and DX1 scope.**\n- **StrictMode:** `pages/spy-axylo.ts` is put between the packed adapter and the packed core through an esbuild plugin. It logs each chart's create and destroy, and which chart's listeners received events.\n- **What the test asserts:**\n  - The log reads exactly create#1, destroy#1, create#2.\n  - The destroyed chart #1 ignores `update`, `append` and `zoom`: still one figure, title unchanged.\n  - Hover events reach chart #2 only.\n  - Unmount adds destroy#2 and leaves `#root` empty. After that, nothing reacts to `update`, resize or the pointer.\n- **Untitled variants:** the npm, script-tag and React snippets each draw without their title and print only AX140.\n- **DX1 scope:** I did not add first-chart examples for the other four chart types. `tests/dist/README.md` and the test header now state that DX1 is established for the documented line chart by all three routes, with only the package-level parts carrying over to the other types.\n\n**22. Fixture isolation and extraction.**\n- **Install by npm:** the hand-written tar extractor and the symlinks are gone. `pack.mjs` now runs `npm install --offline --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund --no-package-lock` in each fixture, from the fixture's own `package.json`. The React fixture now declares `@types/react` and `@types/react-dom`. All of this works offline here.\n- **Environment:** `npm_*` variables are stripped from the install's environment. When the tests were started through npx, one of them (allow-scripts) made the install refuse to run with `EALLOWSCRIPTS`.\n- **Cache fallback:** if the npm cache lacks registry metadata for the third-party packages (a cache filled only by `npm ci`), the install falls back to `--prefer-offline`. That fallback has not happened here.\n- **Guards:** the fixtures still sit under the repository, so isolation is enforced by guards instead:\n  - Node: `tests/dist/guard/node-guard.mjs`, a resolve hook loaded with `--import`; `nodeJson` always uses it.\n  - esbuild: `fixtureGuard` in `pack.mjs`, now on every test build and in `size.mjs`. It immediately caught the CSP module entry's import of the pages directory, which is now explicitly allowed.\n  - TypeScript: the `--listFiles` output, since no option stops TypeScript walking up to the root `node_modules`. I checked: a `paths` mapping does not stop it.\n- **What `isolation.test.ts` asserts:**\n  - npm's lockfile names the packed tarball and its integrity for each of our packages.\n  - There are no links in any fixture `node_modules`.\n  - Without a guard, the undeclared package does resolve into the workspace, so the guards have something to stop.\n  - Declared imports resolve inside each fixture, with and without `--conditions=development`, including the adapter's own `import 'axylo'`.\n  - An undeclared import (`@axylo/react` from the npm and script fixtures, `fast-check` from the React fixture) is stopped by all three guards.\n\n**23. CSP observation.**\n- **Reports:** each page's policy now names its own `report-uri /csp-report/<page>`, and the server keeps reports per page. A last test waits, then requires all 8 pages to have zero reports and no report under an unknown page id.\n- **Negative control:** it waits for exactly 2 reports, requires their directives to be exactly `script-src-attr` and `style-src-attr` with `document-uri` equal to the control page, and the events to name the same two directives.\n- **Stylesheet effect,** on every page:\n  - computed `display: flex` on the figure and `font-weight: 600` on the title;\n  - `--axylo-default-series-1` equal to the light value the packed `axylo.css` declares (#4a94eb), and the first series mark's computed colour equal to it;\n  - a screenshot, decoded by the new dependency-free `tests/dist/png.ts`, with more than 50 pixels of that colour (176 observed);\n  - with the sheet disabled or un-adopted, `display` is no longer `flex`.\n\n**24. Oracles.**\n- **Development-only behaviour:** new `flavours.browser.test.ts`, with a list written by hand from spec 3.5, plan 1.6 and 1.7, and the diagnostics docs. It runs on all four core files and both adapter files:\n  - the AX104 hint prints once in development and never in production;\n  - it reaches `onDiagnostic` and `chart.diagnostics` in both;\n  - the AX140 warning prints once in both;\n  - the adapter's memoize hint appears once in development and never in production.\n- **Token check:** the source-derived check in `conditions.test.ts` is kept but labelled supplementary, with the reviewer's objection stated in its header.\n- **Licence:** `THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md` now has a release inventory of all 16 shipped files, each with origin and licence, plus a Notices section. `licence.test.ts` now asserts:\n  - the inventory equals the tarballs' file lists exactly;\n  - each claimed origin holds: bundle inputs come only from the package's `src`, and the shipped CSS equals the string `theme/css.ts` exports;\n  - any licence or copyright header found in source or shipped files must be quoted in Notices, instead of counting as a failure;\n  - the existing checks (MIT, no dependencies, imports) are kept.\n\n  The notices file now says that third-party code copied into `src` without a header cannot be detected.\n\n### Files I changed or added\n- **Changed:**\n  - `packages/axylo/src/index.ts` (the two specifiers above)\n  - `scripts/pack.mjs`, `scripts/size.mjs`\n  - `THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md`\n  - `tests/dist/{README.md, browser.ts, paths.ts, types.test.ts, isolation.test.ts, conditions.test.ts, csp.browser.test.ts, first-chart.browser.test.ts, licence.test.ts, side-effects.test.ts}`\n  - `tests/dist/pages/{observe.js, page.css}`\n  - the three fixture `package.json` files, and `fixtures/react/tsconfig.json`\n- **New:**\n  - `tests/dist/{guard/node-guard.mjs, png.ts, size.test.ts, text.browser.test.ts, flavours.browser.test.ts}`\n  - `tests/dist/pages/{sentinels.ts, spy-axylo.ts, flavour.ts}`\n  - `fixtures/{npm,react}/tsconfig.nodenext.json`, `fixtures/react/{main-strict.tsx, memo.tsx}`\n\nThe package READMEs and LICENSE files are untouched. No new `.gitignore` lines are needed: the scratch output goes under the already-ignored `fixtures/*/.out/` and `tests/dist/.out/`.\n\n### Not verified\n- The `--prefer-offline` fallback, and fixture installs on a CI runner whose cache was filled by `npm ci`.\n- Windows and macOS.\n- Browsers other than Chrome, and bundlers other than esbuild.\n- The webpack effect of `sideEffects: [\"*.css\"]`.\n- The headless Chrome still running at the end belongs to the other agent's `vitest run packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance`; mine closed.\n\n<!-- Author: Codex gpt-6.2-sol, effort xhigh (observed in the session rollout, thread 01a1ed5c-3882-70a3-95a7-ec73a934e521). -->\n\nThe release checker now blocks on stale provenance, failed suites, skipped citations, and missing evidence. The strengthened Node suite exposed **seven failures in the current library**. Its report separates those failures from the failures introduced by each mutation. [Acceptance report](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/acceptance/report.md) · [Mutation report with exact failing test titles](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/acceptance/REPORT.md)\n\n### Mutation proofs\n\n| Finding | Mutation | Additional failures |\n|---|---|---:|\n| 1 | Bypass dense simplification. The tests supply at least 10 values per device column and require the drawing to discard vertices. | 8 |\n| 2 | Keep the first sample instead of the per-column minimum. The tests check both extrema, including on cumulative area boundaries. | 8 |\n| 3 | Default instants and invalid-zone fallback to UTC. The tests vary the viewer zone across UTC, Los Angeles, and Kolkata. | 21 |\n| 4 | Drop AX103 and AX106 rows from scatter tables. Static data and appends now retain those rows as “No data,” including with `missing: 'zero'`. Diagnostic counts stayed the same. | 6 |\n| 5 | Choose the stack side from the first value in each series. The geometry properties vary signs per cell and include zero and missing values. | 4 |\n| 6 | Force small percent and ratio maxima to half scale. The tests check the first enclosing ticks and the 4% fixture. | 10 |\n| 12 | Remove forced isolated markers. Both line and area fail the coordinate check below 12 px spacing. | 2 |\n\n### Failures left in place\n\n- **F7, four tests.** The automatic domain must end at the first tick at or beyond each extreme. With a maximum reference of `0.1`, the first eligible drawn tick is `0.1`, but the domain ends at `0.2` for line, area, bar, and scatter. The likely source is domain rounding and tick selection. [domain.ts](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/scale/domain.ts:77) · [axes.ts](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/axes.ts:101)\n- **F8, one test.** I counted zero and null entries as slices because F8 calls them slices. Nine entries should trigger AX121 and group two as `Other (2)`. The fixture emits no diagnostic because grouping counts positive arcs. [donut.ts](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/view/donut.ts:33)\n- **F10, two tests.** The axis covers both totals and the geometry checks pass. F10 explicitly requires both totals on the axis and leaves the mixed-sign readout ambiguous. I chose to expose both signed totals to the reader. Area and bar report `4` for `+9/−5` and `0 (incomplete)` for `+5/−5`. [aligned.ts](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/lookup/aligned.ts:109)\n\n### Evidence and verification\n\n- Findings 7–10, 13–14, and 21 are covered by checker tests for explicit waivers, provenance, suite failures, forbidden modifiers, duplicate titles, helper registrations under prefixes, decorated spec IDs, and stable report order. No waivers were granted. Only `--write` changes the report.\n- For finding 11, **L1** uses browser evidence for line, area, bar, and scatter. **L3** uses browser evidence for all five full chart types. **C3** uses browser evidence for all compact kinds. **L8 and AX142** now vary container and caption sizes. **AX143** varies text metrics and width. The only additions I made to `host.ts` were optional viewer zone, locale, text width, and caption line height settings, with the existing defaults. [host.ts](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/core/host.ts)\n- Findings 15–19 added exact diagnostic sets, AX105 boundary cases, all-string AX106, production AX140, broader generators, compact kind metadata, and checks just above and below 12 px. For findings 20 and 22, the matrix separates Chrome accessibility-tree evidence from screen-reader rehearsal, makes **I2/donut** applicable because donut has a legend, and makes **A7/compact** applicable because data can arrive without point focus. The Node quiet-append test passes. Donut toggling, compact browser announcements, screen-reader rehearsal, current browser and packed distribution results, and performance workloads remain unverified.\n\n| Checker inputs | Pass | Fail | Weak | No evidence | Not run | Cannot verify | N/A |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| Reviewer’s archived run | 117 | 11 | 128 | 18 | 21 | 15 | 266 |\n| Updated checker, archived results | 72 | 23 | 121 | 18 | 63 | 15 | 264 |\n| Updated checker, fresh Node results | 5 | 13 | 129 | 18 | 132 | 15 | 264 |\n\nThe requested Vitest run ended with `Test Files 3 failed | 19 passed (22)` and `Tests 7 failed | 661 passed (668)`. Checker tests passed **19/19**, and typecheck passed. The release check exited **1** and recorded that the shared working tree changed during Vitest. I verified that running the checker without `--write` left the report byte-for-byte intact.\n\nScratch files were removed, no test processes were left running, and no project commits or contract changes were made.<!-- Analyst: Codex gpt-6.2-sol, effort max (observed in the session rollout, thread 01a1ed40-1abb-7cc2-8049-290d1bed775e), read-only. Source snapshot 8ab0734 per the analysis. -->\n\n**Keep the feature set and reset DX7.** I would set the next packed-distribution gates at **96 KiB for everything, 80 KiB for `line`, and 2 KiB for the React adapter**. Those are proposed gates. The measured line-only scratch bundle reached **79.79 KiB** with both renderers, streaming, zoom, the table, and keyboard support still in the bundle. With lazy loading, **93 KiB everything and 77 KiB line** are defensible *initial-download* gates. The original 40/25 KiB targets imply a substantially smaller product.\n\n## Measurements\n\nI reproduced the packed measurements from the installed fixtures using the read-only steps in `scripts/size.mjs`. I used esbuild 0.28.2, ES2022 ESM, production conditions, minification, `write: false`, and gzip level 9. For source experiments I froze the source at `8ab0734`, set `__DEV__` to `false`, and substituted code with esbuild `onLoad`. The raw production ESM rebuilt from that snapshot differed from the packed file by 34 minified bytes and 9 gzip bytes. Source deltas below use their own baseline of **99,921 gzip B** for everything and **99,736 gzip B** for the bare `line` export. Source line references are to that snapshot. [Size measurement](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/scripts/size.mjs:33) · [Release build](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/scripts/build.mjs:7) · [Distribution report](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/dist.report.md:60)\n\n| Measured entry | Minified JS B | Download gzip B | Download KiB |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Packed, all exports | 283,389 | 100,093 | 97.75 |\n| Packed, documented `line` example | 282,947 | 99,956 | 97.61 |\n| Packed, bare `line` export | 282,758 | 99,872 | 97.53 |\n| Packed, `validate` alone | 73,998 | 25,556 | 24.96 |\n| Source, `validate` alone | 56,491 | 20,700 | 20.21 |\n| Packed, script tag with linked CSS | 283,869 | 104,274 | 101.83 |\n| Packed, React adapter with React and Axylo external | 2,375 | 1,197 | 1.17 |\n\nThe chart bundles include embedded CSS. The script-tag row also counts its linked CSS. The measured gaps to DX7 are **59,133 B for everything** and **74,356 B for the line example**. [Size cases and accounting](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/scripts/size.mjs:42) · [DX7](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/02-product-spec.md:228)\n\n## Where the bytes are\n\nModule sizes are measured esbuild metafile contributions in **minified B**. Function and table sizes in the middle column are **approximate minified B**, attributed by generated source-map spans. They include nested declarations. Gzip deltas are measured by rebuilding after removing the named behavior and include any callees that then disappear. The deltas cannot be added across rows.\n\n| Source area | Largest functions, tables, and strings | Measured gzip evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Controller** · 23,985 B | `frame` ≈6,499, `update` ≈1,434, `formatSpec` ≈1,034. The frame function coordinates formatting, responsive layout, disclosure, and the atomic publication of marks, lookup, and accessibility state. | Stubbing `frame` removes 27,124 B including downstream stages. Stubbing `update` removes 571 B. [Create and update](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts:492) · [Frame](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts:876) |\n| **Interaction** · 22,278 B | `present` ≈1,342, `build` ≈1,156, `onProxyKey` ≈1,083, `controlItems` ≈882, `placeProxy` ≈880. The shared state links pointer, touch, keyboard, focus, and the displayed revision. | Stubbing `present` removes 1,325 B. Stubbing the whole interaction factory removes 14,272 B including its dependencies. [Factory](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:150) · [Presentation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:360) · [Keyboard](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:543) |\n| **Aligned streaming** · 18,633 B for `store.ts` | `appendInPlace` ≈2,561, `commitBatch` ≈1,664, `batch` ≈1,380, `trim` ≈1,128, `extendSeries` ≈1,062. The shared stream row and aligned buffer modules contribute another 5,944 and 3,307 B respectively. | `appendInPlace` and `batch` ablations each remove about 1,050 B in separate builds. Cutting streaming end to end removes 13,692 B from everything and 11,138 B from the specialized line scratch bundle. [Batch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store.ts:624) · [Append](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store.ts:895) · [Row revisions](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store-rows.ts:275) · [Frame buffers](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store-buffers.ts:23) |\n| **Point and donut streaming** · 7,806 B | `UnalignedStore` ≈3,509, `Points` ≈1,757, `Slices` ≈1,228. The first is shared by the last two. An aligned chart has no use for these classes. | The entire module disappeared from the line scratch metafile once `openStore` directly selected `AlignedStore`. [Point storage](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store-unaligned.ts:87) · [Slice storage](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store-unaligned.ts:175) · [Store constructors](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store-unaligned.ts:424) |\n| **Static data and validation** · 8,692 B normalize, 5,598 B options, 5,715 B x parsing | `XParser` ≈3,237, `resolveOptions` ≈1,240, `pointFrame` ≈1,175, `donutFrame` ≈1,159, `readX` ≈1,074, `alignedFrame` ≈906. The option-name tables include ≈779 B for `messageKeys`. | Stubbing `resolveOptions` removes 1,090 B including helpers. Source `validate` alone is 20,700 B because it runs option resolution, normalization **and** view diagnostics for all types. [Options](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/options.ts:47) · [X parser](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/x.ts:141) · [Frame dispatch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/normalize.ts:465) · [Validation path](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/stages.ts:145) |\n| **Layout and time** · 7,273 B label fitting, 3,461 B layout, 5,107 B time scale | `layoutAxes` ≈1,689, `fitBottom` ≈1,296, `middleText` ≈838. Time-scale `label` and `wallBoundaries` are each ≈660, and the `SUB_YEAR` interval table is ≈543. | Stubbing `fitBottom` removes 720 B. Stubbing temporal ticks, readouts, gap arithmetic, and zone conversion together removes 3,442 B and breaks F2/F4/F6. [Layout](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/layout/index.ts:129) · [Labels](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/layout/labels.ts:363) · [Time intervals](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/scale/time.ts:51) |\n| **Marks and theme** · 9,025 B SVG, 6,307 B Canvas, 4,975 B theme resolution | SVG `createSvgRenderer` ≈3,600, including ≈1,023 for bar-only `patternUrl`. Canvas `createCanvasRenderer` ≈1,633 and `paintRects` ≈1,545. Both use shared geometry. Theme `resolveTheme` ≈1,167 and `validColors` ≈662. The light and dark palette literals together are ≈398. | SVG-only saves 2,190 B. Canvas-only saves 2,975 B. Both keep the HTML text layer. [SVG](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:212) · [Canvas](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/render/canvas.ts:42) · [Shared geometry](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/render/geometry.ts:122) · [Theme](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/theme/resolve.ts:88) |\n| **Styles and text** · 15,140 B stylesheet module, 6,849 B registry, 2,685 B reader messages | The stylesheet template is the largest string. The AX registry table accounts for ≈5,716 B. `defaultMessages` accounts for ≈1,904 B, including the F12 note, summary, keyboard, and table text. | Removing embedded CSS text with the installer retained saves 3,911 B. Replacing registry prose with generic code/field/rows saves 1,866 B. Blanking reader message values saves just 479 B and breaks reader behavior. [Stylesheet](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/theme/css.ts:48) · [AX registry](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/diagnostics/registry.ts:39) · [Reader messages](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/messages/index.ts:8) |\n\n**Shared and feature-specific code.** All full charts need validation, normalization, the figure, measurement and theming, exact lookup, and a reader summary. Axis fitting and references are shared by Cartesian charts. Line and area share dense-line simplification. Area adds `addArea` and `addStackedAreas` in a measured 3,088 B module. Bar adds `barMarks` in a 1,839 B module, plus band projection, horizontal labels, and missing-bar glyphs. Bar and area share stacks. Scatter adds point normalization, its store, point order and a 3,176 B grid module. Donut adds slice normalization, its store, ring layout, `arcMarks`, and its separate legend. The line scratch build eliminated the area and bar scene modules and all the scatter and donut lookup modules. [Line and area dispatch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/scene/index.ts:85) · [Area stacks](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/scene/areas.ts:121) · [Bar marks](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/scene/bars.ts:38) · [Stacks](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/view/index.ts:348) · [Donut legend](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:1149)\n\n**Optional behavior already reachable from every relevant chart.** Table work lives in `buildTable` (≈1,663 minified B), `createTableView` (3,232 B module), and interaction’s open, paginate, and jump functions. The tooltip has a 3,493 B presenter module plus overlay and anchor logic, some shared with keyboard focus. Zoom spans controller `zoom`, focus window arithmetic, and DOM button, wheel, drag, and pinch handlers. Compact reuses the main draw path. Folding compact-only branches to false with CSS and schemas retained removed only **156 measured gzip B**. [Table model](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/reader/table.ts:78) · [Table DOM](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/table.ts:43) · [Table open and jump](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:997) · [Tooltip](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/tooltip.ts:76) · [Zoom arithmetic](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/focus.ts:275) · [Compact layout](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/layout/index.ts:219)\n\n## Why `line` retains everything\n\nThe source metafile showed **zero contributing modules disappearing** when I switched from all exports to the bare `line` export. Only `index.ts` and `core/stages.ts` had lower attributed module byte counts. The production package exports a single prebundled ESM file, but the same result from source establishes the actual dependency problem. `sideEffects: [\"*.css\"]` is already in place. [Package metadata](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/package.json:7) · [Build entry](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/scripts/build.mjs:7)\n\n1. **`line` → `createChart('line', …)` → `createController(kind, …)`.** Area, bar, scatter, and donut call the same functions. The literal passed by each wrapper crosses the same runtime dispatch. The controller keeps its append implementation because every returned `Chart` exposes synchronous `append`, even on a page that has yet to stream anything. Its `openStore` directly references `AlignedStore.fromFrame`, `new PointStore`, and `new DonutStore`. [Entry points](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/index.ts:14) · [Browser host](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/chart.ts:129) · [Store dispatch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts:555) · [Public handle](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts:1427)\n2. **`update` → `resolveOptions` → `normalize` → `buildView`.** `keysForChart[type]` retains all option schemas. `normalize` calls `donutFrame`, `pointFrame`, or `alignedFrame`. `buildView` calls `buildDonut`, does scatter extent and range work, or builds aligned views with bar band and stack branches. Specializing the store alone leaves these static paths reachable. [Option resolution](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/options.ts:209) · [Normalize](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/normalize.ts:465) · [View dispatch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/view/index.ts:304)\n3. **`frame` → axes, scene, lookup, reader, and DOM interaction.** `layoutInput` selects ring, bands, or XY. `buildScene` references arcs, bars, scatter points, area, and line. `buildLookup` switches between donut, scatter, and aligned lookup. `buildReader` calls `buildTable`, which has three frame-shape paths. The interaction factory branches on the same types for keyboard navigation, overlays, swatches, and the donut legend. [Controller dispatch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts:1040) · [Axis dispatch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/axes.ts:79) · [Scene dispatch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/scene/index.ts:85) · [Lookup switch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/lookup/index.ts:8) · [Table switch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/reader/table.ts:78) · [Navigation dispatch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:465)\n4. **`createChart` selects both renderers at runtime.** The private `__renderer` switch directly references both factories. Each factory’s mark switch references every mark painter, including arcs and bar patterns even when the scene can only produce line marks. The chart also directly creates the text layer, DOM figure and interaction layer, and installs styles and watchers. [Renderer choice and browser services](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/chart.ts:60) · [SVG mark switch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:285) · [Canvas mark switch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/render/canvas.ts:160)\n\n**Smallest restructuring that removes those edges.** Give each named chart entry a small private composition that supplies its option schema, normalizer and store opener, view and layout builders, scene and lookup builders, plus the type-specific navigation and reader operations. Keep one controller, one browser host, and one implementation of shared aligned data and Cartesian layout. Pass the aligned store its encoding operations so its re-encoding path can also avoid the all-type resolver. Split the two render switches into mark handlers shared by per-type renderer compositions. A line composition needs paths, points, rules and bands, including the points and rules used by focus. It can omit area fills, bar rectangles and arcs. Keep `validate(type, options)` on a separate, pure dispatch path so importing `line` alone cannot reach it. [Controller view and store calls](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts:416) · [Aligned store re-encoding](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store.ts:460) · [Validation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/index.ts:34)\n\nShip the production and development ESM graphs with those module boundaries intact. The package file allowlist and packed-content tests will need to admit the modules and any chunks. Keep the classic `Axylo` IIFE as a single, self-contained build of all exports. It can retain both renderers and the full interaction layer. The public names, options, handle signatures, and fixed core contracts can stay as they are. [Package allowlist](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/package.json:8) · [Package-content tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/package-contents.test.ts:20) · [Public API](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/public.ts:110)\n\nI tested the dependency result with an in-memory `export { line }` entry. I folded the relevant chart-kind and frame-shape branches to line, replaced `openStore` with a direct aligned call, and removed the unreachable mark cases in each backend. This measured **81,704 gzip B (79.79 KiB)** with both renderers, down **18,032 B**. SVG alone measured **80,321 B (78.44 KiB)**. The metafile confirmed that point/donut stores, the scatter grid and point order, all scatter/donut lookup, and bar/area scene modules disappeared. This models the code the composition can omit. A production refactor will have dispatch and packaging overhead of its own.\n\n## Ranked reductions for everything\n\nThe ranking prioritizes shippable savings. All deltas are gzip B. “Cut” denotes a measured ablation, and effort is estimated.\n\n| Rank | Change and measured saving | Verdict, risk, and effort |\n|---:|---|---|\n| 1 | **Minify the embedded CSS.** Pre-expanding and minifying the packed-equivalent stylesheet saved **1,604 B** in JS. The standalone file went from **4,095 to 2,433 B** gzip. The current script snippet’s duplicate download costs **4,095 B**, or **2,433 B** after CSS minification. | **Do it.** Generate both forms from the same stylesheet. Make the linked file optional in the default snippet, as the README already allows. Moving all CSS to a link adds a setup dependency for DX1 and changes the DX8 CSP and first-render paths. Check L1–L4, A5–A6 and DX6. Small. [Installer](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/theme/styles.ts:29) · [Script snippet](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/examples/first-chart/script.html:3) · [README](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/README.md:48) |\n| 2 | **Keep SVG eager and defer Canvas.** SVG-only cut **2,190 B**. Canvas-only cut **2,975 B**. A real ESM dynamic edge for Canvas saved **1,647 B initially** for everything and **916 B** after line specialization, including split overhead. | **Keep SVG as the default pending the renderer rerun.** Canvas is useful enough to preserve for comparison, especially dense scatter. Decision 0002 voids the old dashboard comparison and calls for dense scatter, zoom, headed GPU and another browser before a final choice. Check F5, A5, L4, P1–P5 and P7. Medium. [Selection](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/chart.ts:62) · [Decision 0002](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md:47) |\n| 3 | **Lazy-load the table.** Splitting only `createTableView` saved **923 B initially** for everything. Cutting its model, presenter, disclosure handlers and jump code together saved **2,923 B**. The latter is the measured amount available before adding loading and availability code. | **Split the presenter first.** Move model construction only after the A4 readiness and focus behavior is tested. Keep the control, table availability, and F12 wording synchronous. Check A2–A4, F2, F5–F6, F12, L8 and DX8. Medium for the presenter, larger for the whole boundary. [Reader model](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/reader/index.ts:148) · [Open, focus and jump](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:997) |\n| 4 | **Remove production instrumentation.** Removing the stage counters, forced-failure logic and benchmark handle block saved **586 B**. `__dev` already disappears. `__AXYLO_BENCH__`, `__bench`, and the forced-stage-failure string were still present in the measured production source bundle. | **Do it with the benchmark owner.** Give the harness its own production-optimized instrumentation build, and run shipped-bundle performance checks as well. Keep AX199 recovery. Check P1–P8 evidence and DX5 failure recovery. Small. [Counters](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts:250) · [Runtime benchmark hook](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts:1445) |\n| 5 | **Shorten diagnostic advice.** Shortening hint text saved **253 B**. Shortening all registry messages while retaining outcome templates saved **774 B** in the scratch build. The **1,866 B** generic-message cut discarded required detail. | **Keep the codes and actionable production messages.** Move expanded examples and explanations into development or the documented code pages. Preserve AX100 lengths, AX101 and AX150 suggestions, chart/field/rows, strict errors and warning delivery. Hints are suppressed in the production *console* but still reach `validate`, `diagnostics` and `onDiagnostic`. Keep each hint’s required meaning and retain reader text and disclosure counts in every build. Check F3, F4, F12, C5, A10 and DX4–DX5. Small to medium. [Registry requirements](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/02-product-spec.md:148) · [Public diagnostic](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/public.ts:163) · [Delivery](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/delivery.ts:50) · [Reader disclosure](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/diagnostics/collector.ts:55) |\n| 6 | **Reuse exact duplicates and trim private class methods.** One consolidation of the message allowlist, searches, clamp, unit label, and identical DOM attribute setters saved **381 B**. Removing store convenience methods and their alternate append wrapper saved **204 B**. Mangling a selected set of internal property names saved **217 B** in a separate build. | **Consolidate exact duplicates.** Preserve the hot search and DOM mutation semantics. Keep names used by user options, `Messages`, diagnostics, CSS, or quoted/dynamic property access stable. Mangling is a low-return follow-up and needs consistent names across chunks. Check F7, A2/A4, DX3–DX6. Small to medium. [Shared searches](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/lookup/search.ts:1) · [Duplicate focus helpers](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/focus.ts:38) · [Duplicate message keys](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/options.ts:55) · [DOM setter](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/figure.ts:74) · [Store wrappers](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store.ts:511) |\n| 7 | **Defer plot keyboard navigation.** Stubbing the proxy handlers, placement, and navigation entry points saved **1,446 B**. I estimate **800–1,200 B** net after a synchronous shell and loader. | **Keep eager for the first release.** The proxy supplies native focus and screen-reader input, and shares the active datum with pointer and streaming. Deferring the first keystroke or announcement is a material regression. Check A2–A3, A7, A10 and I3. Medium to large. [Proxy creation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:220) · [Proxy keys](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:543) · [Revision remapping](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:1252) |\n| 8 | **Separate streaming from static normalization internally.** The **13,692 B** streaming cut removed `append`, the store dispatch, pause/follow logic, and stream seeds from static normalization. | **Keep synchronous streaming in the standard entry.** Loading storage on first `append` would break synchronous validation and ordered calls. Full renormalization per append would put P3/P7 and pause/retention behavior at risk. Share draft/commit and re-encoding logic where the aligned and unaligned paths agree. Large for meaningful savings. [Stream seed](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/normalize.ts:465) · [Aligned transaction](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store.ts:471) · [Unaligned transaction](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store-unaligned.ts:385) · [Commit contract](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:107) |\n| 9 | **Keep calendar and zone arithmetic.** The broad temporal cut saved **3,442 B**. Stubbing just inverse zone conversion and the offset search saved **228 B**. | `Intl` already supplies formatting and zone data. It does not supply strict three-kind classification, civil tick stepping, or the inverse mapping needed to distinguish repeated and skipped wall times. The parser, gap engine and scale already share `utcFields` and zone conversion. Keep them for F2, F4 and F6. Large to redesign. [Parsing](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/x.ts:64) · [Gap arithmetic](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/steps.ts:39) · [Zone conversion](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/scale/zone.ts:42) · [Intl cache](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/format/intl.ts:6) |\n| 10 | **Keep the scatter grid.** Stubbing `scatterGrid` saved **1,308 B** and disabled scatter pointer picking. | A flat scan can implement the same radius, later-row tie and coincidence rules, with a different cost on dense and clustered data. Keep the grid for I1/P4 and the scatter type matrix. The line split already removes all its bytes from line. Medium to replace and requalify. [Grid, grouping and split](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/lookup/scatter-grid.ts:33) · [Picker](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/lookup/scatter.ts:153) · [Type matrix](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/02-product-spec.md:188) |\n| 11 | **Leave typed-array growth alone for size.** Sharing the simple allocate/copy step between scalar observations and frame buffers saved **40 minified B** and **added 24 gzip B**. | `BufferWindow` already centralizes reserve, compact and copy-on-write rules. Preserve independent inspected previews, borrowed columns and retained row provenance. Check DX2, DX5 and P7. Small experiment, larger correctness surface. [Shared window](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store-window.ts:3) · [Scalar observations](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store.ts:83) · [Parallel buffers](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store-buffers.ts:63) |\n\nTwo other cuts show the cost of the remaining feature paths. The tooltip presenter and its presentation branch saved **1,570 B** when stubbed, and disabling zoom handlers and controller zoom saved **989 B** in a separate cut. Keep both for I1/I3. The compact branch cut saved **156 B** with CSS and schemas retained, so C1–C5 are inexpensive to keep. [Tooltip presentation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:360) · [Zoom handlers](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:643) · [Compact criteria](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/02-product-spec.md:200)\n\nThe duplication audit also found distinct checks that should stay distinct. `sameValue` compares option content recursively, `sameKey` compares stage inputs, and the store tests whether a normalized seed is reusable. Field checks recur against initial data, re-encoded retained data, and incoming rows. The `validateOptions` helper in `data/validate.ts` contributes zero bytes, so deleting it would save zero. Text uses DOM setters, and I found no duplicated general-purpose HTML escaping pipeline. The table’s two regex escapes are local to parsing a localized jump value. [Option equality](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/options.ts:66) · [Cache keys](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/axes.ts:246) · [Seed check](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store.ts:30) · [Retargeting](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/options.ts:196) · [Append field check](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store.ts:904) · [Unused validator](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/validate.ts:6) · [Table parser](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:997)\n\n### What lazy loading changes\n\nI built actual in-memory ESM chunks for the Canvas and table-presenter edges. With compiled CSS, their **initial** totals were **93.45 KiB everything / 76.43 KiB line**, and their **all-chunk** totals were **97.55 / 79.54 KiB**, summing the gzip size of each file. The unused eager Canvas re-export and table value import initially caused those modules to land in eagerly imported shared chunks. Removing both static edges fixed that. Compiling the two dynamic edges into one classic IIFE kept one output and **added 530 B** to the comparable IIFE. The single-file script build should keep eager implementations. [Renderer barrel](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/render/index.ts:1) · [Table import](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:32) · [IIFE build](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/scripts/build.mjs:11)\n\nThe npm path can work without author configuration if the build emits relative URLs and deploys every chunk alongside the entry. The existing `script-src 'self'` policy can load same-origin chunks. Cross-origin module hosting needs the applicable CSP origin and CORS. For the table, keep the disclosure control and accessible name eager, resolve the newest displayed revision when the chunk arrives, and make one activation sufficient to open and focus the table. A full model split can use a private synchronous availability check while retaining the pure `ReaderModel` contract for its existing callers. Any keyboard split needs an eager focus proxy and first-action handling. Test throttled loading, failed fetch and retry, updates during loading, and destruction before completion. [Current CSP test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/csp.browser.test.ts:46) · [Reader contract](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts:660) · [Table and keyboard criteria](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/02-product-spec.md:137)\n\n## Realistic release sizes\n\n| Scenario | Everything | Line only | Confidence and basis |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| **Eager restructuring, full behavior** | **94–97 KiB estimated** | **76–81 KiB estimated** | Medium-high. Type composition alone should leave everything near the current size. The combined CSS, bench, and shorter-hint scratch variants measured **95.16 / 77.42 KiB**. The range allows for real composition and packing overhead. |\n| **Lazy table, keyboard, and second renderer, initial download** | **89–93 KiB estimated** | **73–77 KiB estimated** | Medium. The actual Canvas and table DOM split with minified CSS measured **93.45 / 76.43 KiB** before the benchmark/text changes or deeper table and keyboard extraction. A combined cut with all three boundaries absent measured **88.12 / 72.17 KiB** before adding their loaders and eager shells. Keep an aggregate-download measurement as well. |\n| **Product reduced to fit 40/25 KiB** | **20.07 KiB measured** for a caller-prepared draw path | **15.81 KiB measured** for the corresponding line path | High confidence in the size of those scratch entries. They take a prepared `View` and layout inputs, and ship layout, SVG, HTML labels and minified CSS. The caller owns input processing, sizing, and the reader experience. They have no chart controller or live interaction. |\n\nThe old targets are **unsuitable for the required release scope**. A single combined cut that removed streaming, table and plot keyboard behavior, tooltip, zoom, key temporal paths, scatter picking, and Canvas still weighed **67.06 KiB everything / 54.96 KiB line**. Even a one-shot SVG scratch entry that took raw inputs and retained normalization, validation, axis fitting and CSS, while leaving out the chart controller and reader interactions, measured **45.41 / 38.80 KiB**. To ship at 40/25 with this architecture, the product would also have to narrow or delegate input handling and axis behavior. Requiring prepared numeric or category data, with application-owned temporal parsing, diagnostics and accessible readouts, is an example of a scope that fits. It gives up parts of F3–F4, A1–A4, DX2–DX5 and I6. [Release scope](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/02-product-spec.md:46) · [Pipeline and fidelity](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:169)\n\nPhase 1 measured **uPlot 23 KiB**, **D3 35 KiB**, **Chart.js 81 KiB**, **Nivo 129 KiB + React**, **Recharts 140 KiB + React**, **Highcharts 143 KiB with accessibility**, **MUI X 160 KiB + React**, **ApexCharts 289 KiB**, **ECharts 373 KiB**, and **Plotly 1,450 KiB**. These are the documented-import harness measurements, with CSS counted as text and React external where applicable. Phase 1 did not measure the smallest modular builds. [Phase 1 sizes and method](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/01-landscape.md:95)\n\nuPlot’s harness supplied dimensions, time units and a bar path, and got a pointer legend with no donut or data keyboard navigation. D3’s chart UI was authored for the harness. Chart.js covered the chart tasks and used a time adapter, but its default accessibility tree had no chart content. Highcharts with its accessibility module is the closest comparison for generated summaries and keyboard navigation. Recharts, ApexCharts and MUI X offered keyboard navigation in the tested configurations, with different focus and announcement behavior. No tested configuration offered a data table without a further module, and none gave the dirty-input field and row reporting Axylo requires. That makes 23 KiB a comparison for a narrower plotting product and 81–143 KiB a more useful context for Axylo’s proposed full gate. [Interaction and accessibility observations](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/01-landscape.md:250) · [Harness setup by library](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/01-landscape.md:308) · [Dirty input findings](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/01-landscape.md:7)\n\n## Work plan\n\n| Order and owner | Expected saving | Evidence required |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. **Core, data, view, scene, lookup, reader, render, distribution** · Compose each chart and separate `validate`. | **~18,000 B line**, estimated from the measured scratch delta. Budget approximately zero saving for everything from composition alone. | On packed ESM, assert that `line` excludes the point/donut stores and other types’ lookup and mark modules. Run typecheck, all five type-matrix cases, dense-line properties, strict create/update/append, packed Node `validate`, and all first-chart snippets. [Type matrix tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/type-matrix.browser.test.ts:42) · [Dense properties](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/acceptance/f-dense.test.ts:49) · [Packed Node tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/node.test.ts) |\n| 2. **Theme, build, docs** · Compile CSS once and update the default script snippet to request one copy. | **~1,600 B** per eager chart build. A separate **4,095 B** script-page saving at the current CSS size. | Compare packed pages with embedded and linked CSS under real CSP headers. Run forced-colors, dark-theme, text-spacing, reflow, and no-overlap cases. [CSP tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/tests/dist/csp.browser.test.ts:46) · [Renderer theme tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/render/theme.browser.test.ts:170) · [Reflow suite](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a6-a10.browser.test.ts) |\n| 3. **Core, bench, diagnostics, data** · Move instrumentation and consolidate exact duplicates. Shorten author advice after checking each affected code. | **~1,000–1,500 B estimated** across the selected changes, with overlap. | Run registry and delivery tests, production F12 in the figure and accessibility tree, strict rejection, and AX199 recovery. Check the benchmark against its existing readiness and revision assertions. [Registry tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/diagnostics/registry.test.ts) · [Production F12](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/acceptance/production.test.ts:19) · [Browser disclosure](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/f12-note.browser.test.ts:30) |\n| 4. **Render and distribution** · Rerun decision 0002, then split the alternate backend in ESM. | **~1,650 B everything / ~900 B line initially**, estimated from the split measurement. | Preserve SVG/Canvas pixel agreement and compare dense scatter, resize, theme, streaming and memory. Test the packed optional chunk and ordinary chart startup under CSP. [Pixel agreement](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/render/render.browser.test.ts:119) · [Renderer rerun requirements](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md:55) |\n| 5. **Reader, DOM, core** · Split the table presenter, then check whether the remaining table model is worth splitting. Keep keyboard eager unless the first-action tests pass under delayed loading. | **~900 B estimated** for the presenter. The whole table boundary has **2,923 B measured gross** available. Keyboard has **1,446 B measured gross** available. | Test first activation and focus return, jumping to a far row, a current zoom window, no dashboard reflow, keyboard entry and exit, and focus through streaming eviction. Repeat under delayed chunks and CSP. [Table acceptance](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a4-table.browser.test.ts:224) · [Keyboard acceptance](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a2-keyboard.browser.test.ts) · [Snapshot invariants](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/core/snapshot.test.ts:290) |\n| 6. **Release coordinator** · Measure the packed npm, IIFE and React cases and freeze DX7. | Proposed gates **96/80/2 KiB** eager, or **93/77 KiB** for initial npm downloads if the lazy split ships. | Run the full unit and browser suites, including the 494 Node acceptance cases in the cited run, and feed their results to the release checker. Record initial and all-chunk totals separately. [Acceptance runner](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/acceptance/README.md:3) · [Archived Node acceptance result](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core3.fix.report.md:96) |\n\nI made no repository edits and propose no changes to `public.ts` or `core/contracts.ts`. I ran the size experiments and in-memory Node smoke checks below. The browser behavior of the scratch variants, renderer performance, and the full acceptance suites remain unverified in this analysis. I treated DX7’s lazy scenario as a proposed initial-download metric, with eventual downloads accounted for separately.\n\n```text\nbaseline: dirty diagnostics/notes/table, zoom-follow append/keep and compact checks passed\nline scratch: dirty diagnostics/notes/table, zoom-follow append/keep and compact checks passed\nDOM-free production validate: dirty fixture codes passed\n```\n\n### Five largest practical wins\n\n| Win | Measured gzip saving | Applies to |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Compose and tree-shake `line` | **18,032 B** | Line-only ESM scratch, both renderers retained |\n| Request the stylesheet once | **4,095 B** at the current CSS size | Documented script-tag page |\n| Defer the table | **2,923 B gross** for the full boundary, **923 B net initially** for the tested presenter split | Everything, initial ESM |\n| Defer Canvas and keep SVG eager | **2,190 B gross** if omitted, **1,647 B net initially** with the tested split | Everything, initial ESM |\n| Minify the embedded stylesheet | **1,604 B** | Everything, eager production JS |","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat docs/decisions/0004-tooltip-and-focused-text.md; sed -n '137,255p' acceptance/report.md; tail -n 125 reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/acceptance-browser.fix.report.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat docs/decisions/0004-tooltip-and-focused-text.md; sed -n '137,255p' acceptance/report.md; tail -n 125 reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/acceptance-browser.fix.report.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Decision 0004: where the tooltip goes, and when the focused point's text changes\n\nStatus: decided by the coordinator on 2026-11-18 after the second review of the interaction layer (`reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core3.interaction.review.md`, new findings 3 and 4). Not rehearsed with a screen reader or a screen magnifier; both parts need that before they are called verified.\n\n## 1. The tooltip is anchored to the datum and never takes the pointer (revision 2)\n\nRevision 1 (placed across the reading axis, offset from the pointer, interactive while hovered) was implemented three times and still failed the acceptance tests: because the tooltip's position followed the pointer it moved away when approached, because it took pointer events it stalled the readout or swallowed the click whenever a move landed on it, and it could cover controls and the focus ring. Revision 2 removes the causes instead of tuning distances.\n\n**Problem.** WCAG 1.4.13 requires content shown on hover to stay when the pointer moves onto it. Spec I1 requires the readout to name the datum at the pointer on every move, the tooltip not to cover the active mark, to stay inside the viewport, and to fall back to a fixed readout when it cannot fit. On a line chart the whole plot picks, so anything that takes pointer events inside the plot blocks picking.\n\n**Rules.**\n\n1. **Position depends on the datum only.** For the datum on show, the tooltip is placed against the bounding box of that datum's active marks (all series at that x for line, area and bar; the point for scatter; the slice for donut), enlarged by the focus ring's width. The pointer's own position never enters the computation, so the tooltip does not move while the datum is unchanged, and it cannot run away from an approaching pointer.\n2. **Candidates, in order:** above the box, below it, then to the right and to the left of it (for horizontal bars: right, left, above, below). Along the other axis it is centred on the box and shifted only as far as needed. A candidate is valid when the tooltip lies wholly inside the plot rectangle, inside the viewport and inside every clipping ancestor, and overlaps neither the box nor any control. The first valid candidate is used. For a donut the first candidate is the hole, when the tooltip fits inside it.\n3. **A hovered tooltip never takes pointer events.** It is `pointer-events: none`. The plot keeps receiving every move, so picking never stalls at any speed, and a click goes to the plot and selects the datum under the pointer. Moving the pointer onto the tooltip therefore cannot hide it: on line, area and bar the tooltip stays for as long as the pointer's position along the reading axis still picks the same datum, which a straight approach across the reading axis does; on scatter and donut, while the pointer is inside the tooltip's rectangle the datum is held (computed from coordinates in the plot's own move handler, not by letting the tooltip take the event).\n4. **Rows are not controls while hovered.** A hovered tooltip is text. Clicking or tapping the plot pins the tooltip for that datum; a pinned tooltip takes pointer events, stays until Escape, a click elsewhere, or its datum leaves the displayed frame, and its rows are buttons of at least 24 × 24 CSS px (WCAG 2.5.8) that select a series. Keyboard focus shows the same tooltip by the same placement; it is never over the focus ring because the box includes the ring.\n5. **Readout mode is decided by layout, not by hover.** When the plot is narrower than 240 CSS px or shorter than 140 CSS px, the figure is in readout mode: a strip of two text lines is reserved under the plot in the layout, and the content that would be in the tooltip is written there as running text (\"x: series value; series value; +N more\"), wrapping to the two lines and ending in \"+N more\" when it does not fit. The strip is part of the figure's flow: it covers nothing and nothing covers it. In readout mode there is no floating tooltip. A pinned datum makes the strip's entries buttons of 24 px height.\n6. **When no candidate is valid outside readout mode** (a tooltip taller than the room around a tall stack, for example), rows are dropped from the end and counted in \"+N more\" until a candidate is valid; if none is with one row, the tooltip is placed inside the plot at the candidate that overlaps the box least. This is the only case in which the active mark may be partly covered, and the table still has every value.\n7. Escape dismisses a hovered or pinned tooltip without moving the pointer, until the pointer reaches a different datum.\n\nRejected: revision 1; an interactive hovered tooltip with a \"safe triangle\" (still ambiguous when the reading axis and the approach coincide); always using the readout (loses the association between the values and the mark on plots that have room).\n\nWhat \"can itself be hovered\" (spec I1) means under this decision, for the tests: the pointer can be moved straight from the datum onto the tooltip's rectangle, and the tooltip is still shown, for the same datum, when the pointer is inside it. It does not mean the tooltip is the hit-test target.\n\n## 2. The focused point's text changes only for a reason the reader would name\n\n**Problem.** The plot's keyboard proxy is a native range whose value text describes the focused point, ending with its position (\"Point 4 of 120\"). While data streams in, the count changes on every append, so the text of a focused control changes without the reader acting. Spec A7 forbids unrequested announcements; whether a given screen reader speaks such a change cannot be tested here.\n\n**Rule.** The range's bounds and numeric value are always kept true to the displayed frame, silently. Its value text is rewritten only when:\n\n1. the reader moves (a key, an assistive adjustment, a pointer or touch selection that takes focus),\n2. the focused datum's own values change (the same x and series now read differently),\n3. the focused datum leaves the frame (the text must then say where focus went), or\n4. the plot regains focus.\n\nAn append that only changes the count or the ordinal of an unchanged datum does not rewrite the text. The position in the text can therefore be out of date until the next of the four events; it is recomputed then.\n\nTo check with a screen reader when one is available (added to decision 0001's list): idle appends with the plot focused are silent; an eviction of the focused point is spoken once; an increment immediately after an append lands on the expected neighbour and reads the current count.\n\n- **L1** · Weak · line, area, bar, scatter, donut · Browser checks use fixed fixtures. Generated labels, late fonts and any historical text-zoom failures require a current browser run.\n- **F2** · Weak · compact · Model checks all three variants. Rendered gap checks exercise line and area, leaving the compact bar’s missing-versus-zero mark unchecked.\n- **F3** · Weak · line, area, bar, scatter, donut · The model asserts diagnostic delivery, effects and drawn output for its cases. The registry still lacks a complete code-by-type check of level and reader outcome, including late diagnostics across types.\n  - Weak · compact · Applicable Node registry fixtures run in compact line, area and bar. Painted contrast, DOM outcomes, and code-by-type coverage in every build still need browser evidence.\n- **F10** · Fail · area, bar\n  - Weak · area, bar · Per-cell signs, zero and missing values exercise both sides and the axis bounds. The reader model currently yields only the algebraic total for the mixed-sign fixture.\n  - Weak · compact · Node checks both sides and missing height for both compact variants. The incomplete summary has no current rendered accessibility-tree check.\n- **F8** · Fail · donut\n  - Weak · donut · The zero/null limit fixture checks whether every input slice counts. The current implementation counts only slices with arcs.\n- **F5** · Weak · line · Node proves simplification on both device-pixel ratios and several zooms, including a dropped sample accessible by keyboard and table. Browser evidence for the current tree is pending.\n  - Weak · area · Node checks simplified cumulative boundaries, gaps on the component stroke and keyboard/table access to a dropped area sample. The cited browser test uses an unstacked outlier fixture.\n  - Weak · compact · Node checks simplified line and cumulative area geometry at two device-pixel ratios. Dense compact rendering in a browser is unverified.\n- **T-dense-line** · Weak · line · Node proves simplification on both device-pixel ratios and several zooms, including a dropped sample accessible by keyboard and table. Browser evidence for the current tree is pending.\n- **T-dense-area** · Weak · area · Node checks simplified cumulative boundaries, gaps on the component stroke and keyboard/table access to a dropped area sample. The cited browser test uses an unstacked outlier fixture.\n- **F7** · Fail · line, area, bar, scatter\n  - Weak · line, area, bar, scatter · Both automatic ends are compared with the first enclosing drawn ticks, including small declared percent and ratio maxima. Zoom is modeled in Node.\n  - Weak · compact · All compact variants check data/reference extent and exact 4% or 0.04 bounds. Compact has no drawn ticks or zoom.\n- **F6** · Weak · line, area, bar, scatter, donut, compact · Localized model strings, independent tick formatting where applicable and formatter fallback are checked. No browser test establishes locale selection from the page and the localized output across the reader surfaces.\n- **A3** · Weak · line, area, bar, scatter, donut · Chrome can verify the focused accessibility-tree name and live-region text after each key. Current evidence checks DOM state and modeled readout text.\n- **A4** · Weak · line · The browser checks one neighboring chart, leaving the 24-chart layout workload unverified.\n  - Weak · area, bar, scatter, donut · Browser pagination and jump reach row 229. The 24-chart layout and million-row jump workload are unverified for this type.\n- **A5** · Weak · bar · Separation and the palette are checked. The default two-series fixture does not establish a series-specific cue beyond color and separation.\n  - Weak · donut · Slice separation and the palette are checked. No assertion identifies each slice without color by mapping it to its text.\n  - Weak · compact · Rendered palette and dash checks use compact line. Compact bar fails the non-color cue check recorded under C4.\n- **L2** · Weak · line, area, bar, scatter, donut · Light and dark computed colors, font sizes and focus contrast are sampled. Text painted inside marks and its actual pixel background are unverified.\n  - Weak · compact · The fixture has no visible chart text, axes or focus. Its computed mark colors are checked against the resolved background, without a rendered-pixel contrast check.\n- **L8** · Weak · line, area, bar, scatter, donut · The Node model varies width, container height and caption height. A browser must establish the one-line summary and controls in the resulting figure.\n- **L3** · Weak · line, area, bar, scatter, donut · Width changes, automatic height and a hidden start are checked. The chart height response to an independent resize and the title/control/table flow are unverified.\n- **L4** · Weak · line, area, bar, scatter, donut, compact · The DOM exercises one body class toggle (sampled after two frame callbacks) and initial forced-colors. It does not prove the one-frame bound for runtime media, html/body inline style and data-theme changes.\n- **I1** · Weak · line, area, bar, scatter · Placement, pointer input and Escape pass sampled checks. The suites do not measure p95 input-to-readout latency or verify hover persistence along a pointer path.\n  - Weak · donut · Placement and Escape are sampled. The donut has no forced narrow readout test or p95 input-to-readout measurement.\n- **I2** · Pending · donut · Donut has a legend under F8 and the toggle rule does not exempt slices. Click, Enter, Space and the pressed state need a donut legend test.\n- **I3** · Weak · line, area, bar, scatter · The four mechanisms are exercised. The click sequence never activates Zoom out, and the wheel guard checks Ctrl only.\n- **A7** · Weak · donut · The browser checks live-region quiet and focus identity. The focused accessible name/value stability case excludes donut.\n  - Weak · compact · A quiet append is verified in all three Node variants. Browser live-region and 24-chart checks are pending. The point-focus requirement applies where the chart has point navigation.\n- **I5** · Weak · line, area, bar, scatter, donut · The current browser tests check first render, reduced motion and a 24-chart filter against the 200 ms budget. Current browser results are pending.\n  - Weak · compact · Current browser citations cover first render and the 200 ms update case in the tested compact fixture. A 24-chart compact filter run is unverified.\n- **A8** · Weak · bar · Checks axe across the listed states for categorical bars. The continuous-axis bar’s zoomed state is outside these fixtures.\n  - Weak · compact · The axe fixtures exercise compact line. Compact area and bar are unverified across the same states.\n- **A10** · Weak · line, area, bar, scatter, donut, compact · The tests supply every known override but scan only selected English strings and surfaces in the tested states. Per-message coverage, including unvisited controls and states, is incomplete.\n- **A9** · No evidence · line, area, bar, scatter, donut, compact · A published WCAG 2.2 AA conformance table lists each applicable criterion, its test, author obligations and limits.\n- **F9** · Weak · compact · Rendered eight-color checks use compact line only, and the twelve-series case checks model bindings rather than distinct rendered combinations in every compact variant.\n- **L5** · Weak · line, area · Dense, sparse and isolated examples are checked. They do not straddle the provisional 12 px cutoff.\n- **C2** · Weak · compact · Baseline, sorted geometry, dirty data and static behavior are covered. Dense rendered compact strokes, compact bar gap styling and live scheme transitions for all three variants remain open.\n- **DX1** · Weak · area, bar, scatter, donut, compact · Only the line has documented first-chart snippets proven in all three packed installation modes.\n- **DX2** · Weak · line, area, bar, scatter, compact · The fixture compares one logical series and one bad row. Multiple-series layouts and diagnostics whose caller indexes differ by form remain unchecked.\n- **DX3** · Weak · area, bar, scatter, donut, compact · Packed type fixtures check specific counterexamples for selected types. The full set of applicable typed and untyped counterparts is unverified for this type.\n  - Weak · line · Packed fixtures check bad line fields, wide y with series and an inapplicable option. The typed text-value failure is exercised on bar, rather than along with the line runtime case.\n- **DX4** · Weak · line, area, bar, scatter, donut, compact · Runtime diagnostics are sampled, with registry cases under F3. A documentation page and verified link for every code are still missing.\n- **DX5** · Weak · line, area, bar, scatter, donut, compact · Sequences and cancellation are checked. Heap retention over 1,000 create–destroy cycles remains unmeasured under P7.\n- **DX6** · Weak · line, area, bar, scatter, donut, compact · The model checks named colors against a changing series set. No 24-tile test establishes cross-chart color stability together with inherited font and color and documented CSS font and spacing overrides.\n- **DX7** · Fail · line, area, bar, scatter, donut, compact\n- **DX8** · Weak · area, bar, scatter, donut, compact · Packed CSP pages draw these charts and Node runs without DOM globals. React StrictMode replay and hydration are exercised only on Line.\n- **DX9** · No evidence · line, area, bar, scatter, donut, compact · Follow semantic versioning and the deprecation policy; previous documented examples still compile and run.\n- **DX10** · Weak · line · The three first-chart examples compile or run as applicable. The rest of the runnable documentation has no corresponding CI build and render.\n  - Weak · area, bar, scatter, donut, compact · Packed consumer fixtures compile calls, without a CI build and render of the runnable documentation examples.\n- **DX12** · No evidence · line, area, bar, scatter, donut, compact · A coding agent uses published docs to make a first chart that passes reader criteria on trap fixtures.\n- **P1** · Pending · line · No valid acceptance measurement. Earlier first-chart comparisons were exploratory.\n- **P2** · Pending · line · No valid acceptance measurement at both point counts. See decision 0002 for limits of the comparisons.\n- **P3** · Pending · line · No valid acceptance measurement of the specified task and presented-frame pacing workload.\n- **P4** · Pending · line, area, bar, scatter · No valid measurement of p95 input-to-matching-frame latency.\n- **P5** · Pending · line · No valid acceptance measurement. The earlier 24-chart run showed fallback text and drew no marks.\n- **P6** · Pending · line, area, bar, scatter, donut, compact · No valid P6 budget run is recorded. The available packed size measurement and target verdict are under DX7.\n- **P7** · Pending · line, area, bar, scatter, donut, compact · No valid owned-bytes and 1,000-cycle heap measurement. The earlier dashboard memory run was voided.\n- **P8** · Pending · compact · No valid 40-tile main-thread measurement.\n\n## What cannot be verified here\n\n- **A3** · line, area, bar, scatter, donut · Record the screen-reader rehearsal on NVDA with Chrome and Firefox, VoiceOver with macOS Safari and iOS, and TalkBack with Chrome, including touch gesture reachability.\n- **A6** · line, area, bar, scatter, donut · Chrome checks 320 CSS px and text-spacing overrides. Firefox and Safari results are absent, and the Chrome fixture uses a narrow viewport rather than actual 400% browser zoom.\n- **I4** · line, area, bar, scatter, donut · Chrome touch emulation provides tap, scroll and target-size evidence. Real phones are needed for native touch behavior.\n- **GPU** · Hardware rasterization needs a headed, GPU-backed run. Archived headless Chrome renderer comparisons cannot establish GPU performance. [docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md](../docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md)\n\n## Evidence that exists but was not run for this report\n\nThese files have citations with missing or skipped assertions in the supplied results.\n\n- L1/line, L1/area, L1/bar, L1/scatter, L1/donut, F3/line, F3/area, F3/bar, F3/scatter, F3/donut, L2/line, L2/area, L2/bar, L2/scatter, L2/donut, L2/compact, L7/line, L7/area, L7/bar, L7/scatter, L7/donut, L7/compact, L8/line, L8/area, L8/bar, L8/scatter, L8/donut, L3/line, L3/area, L3/bar, L3/scatter, L3/donut, L4/line, L4/area, L4/bar, L4/scatter, L4/donut, L4/compact, L6/line, L6/area, L6/bar, L6/scatter, L6/donut, L5/line, L5/area, C2/compact · [packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/l-legibility.browser.test.ts](../packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/l-legibility.browser.test.ts)\n- L1/line · [packages/axylo/test/browser/render/review.browser.test.ts](../packages/axylo/test/browser/render/review.browser.test.ts)\n- C4/compact, F1/compact, F11/compact, C1/compact, C2/compact, C3/compact, C5/compact · [packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/compact.browser.test.ts](../packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/compact.browser.test.ts)\n- F1/area, F1/bar · [packages/axylo/test/scale/domain.test.ts](../packages/axylo